    {
      "sha": "b6ad1f2c3d0616e83e548efa7afed0107af122d8",
      "subject": "feat(mcp): compact input schema profile for size-capped providers (kimi-cli/Moonshot) (#1514)",
      "body": "feat(mcp): compact input schema profile for size-capped providers (kimi-cli/Moonshot) (#1514)\n\n* feat(mcp): add compact input schema profile for size-capped providers\n\nMoonshot's function-calling API rejects a request while any single tool\nparameters schema exceeds its literal serialized size cap, so clients\nsuch as kimi-cli fail every request while the default catalog is\nattached (#1484).\n\nAdd an opt-in REA_MCP_INPUT_SCHEMA_PROFILE=compact server presentation.\nEach advertised input schema is rendered within a 13,312-byte budget,\nmeasured to sit below the observed provider cap: annotation prose\n(nested descriptions, examples, defaults, titles) is dropped, repeated\nannotation-free subschemas are shared through schema-local $defs/$ref\nreferences the provider validates without expansion, and the three\nstructurally oversized schemas are advertised in a documented reduced\nform. The canonical catalog, tool set, output schemas, and server-side\ncanonical validation are unchanged; the default full profile is\nbyte-identical to today's advertisement.\n\nBoundary tests pin every compact schema to the budget, keep them valid\nJSON Schema that still accepts every canonical example, hold reduced\npresentations at three or fewer, and verify canonical rejection still\nguards a permissive advertisement. Verified against the real Moonshot\nAPI through kimi-cli: prompts succeed with the compact catalog attached\nwhere the default catalog fails every request with 400.\n\n* fix(mcp): preserve compact schemas through Kimi conversion\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
