      "sha": "6e0d5d64d3d5b480e370cdaf35f80545913e4093",
      "subject": "feat(ghidra): admit set_address_name and set_addresses_names (#1635)",
      "body": "feat(ghidra): admit set_address_name and set_addresses_names (#1635)\n\n* feat(ghidra): admit set_address_name and set_addresses_names\n\nGhidra sessions rejected the existing naming tools with \"operation is not declared by this provider\", so agents had to use annotate_native_function, which returns a complete function dossier per rename and cannot name data addresses.\n\nThe Java bridge now names a local function entry (namespace-aware, like annotate_native_function) or creates/renames the primary label at any other mapped address, verifying readback. set_addresses_names applies each address in its own transaction and reports per-address booleans. Both are mutating capabilities: excluded from read-only (Windows P0) handshakes and from snapshot replay.\n\n* fix(ghidra): normalize qualified symbol names and reject empty leaves\n\nShare namespace-aware leaf normalization across functions and primary labels. Invalid qualified leaves now return false for naming operations without marking the provider unavailable; function annotations retain typed invalid-input errors.\n\nExtend real CLI/MCP namespace verification with namespaced globals, readback replay, failed-name rollback, mixed batches, and capability checks.\n\nValidation: 75 focused tests and npm run check pass. Ghidra 12.1.4/JDK 21 namespace verification passes on macOS arm64 with unchanged source bytes and complete cleanup. The full host lane passes this workflow before the existing entry-alias fixture fails because the compiler places its interior label at the function entry.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
