      "subject": "fix(install): describe automatic setup and guard truncated downloads (#1902)",
      "body": "fix(install): describe automatic setup and guard truncated downloads (#1902)\n\n* fix(install): describe automatic setup and guard truncated downloads\n\nThe help text said the installer only installs the CLI and that\n`rea setup` must be run separately, but the installer starts\n`rea setup` interactively whenever a terminal is available. Describe\nthat behaviour and the --no-setup/--no-prompt opt-out.\n\nWrap the installer body in `main` and call it on the last line, so a\ntruncated `curl | bash` download fails to parse instead of executing a\npartial script. Behaviour is otherwise unchanged.\n\nAdd an installer scenario that runs a copy truncated right after the\nnpm install line and requires that it prints nothing and never invokes\nnpm; it fails against the previous script.\n\nValidation: bash -n, shellcheck 0.10.0, installScript boundary tests.\n\n* fix(install): require the closing token before running main\n\nWrapping the installer in main() kept a truncated download from running\na partial body, but the last line was a bare `main \"$@\"`. If the piped\ndownload stopped right after `main` (or `main `), bash saw a complete\ncommand at EOF and ran main with no positional parameters. Under the\ndocumented `curl ... | bash -s -- --dry-run` (or --version / --no-setup)\npath that silently dropped the caller's flags, so a dry run could resolve\nthe latest release, run npm, and start setup.\n\nThe call now sits in a brace group:\n\n  {\n    main \"$@\"\n    exit $?\n  }\n\nBash does not run the group until it has read the closing brace, so\nevery truncation inside it is a syntax error and nothing executes; a\ntruncation before the group leaves only function definitions. A comment\nin install.sh records why the bare call is not enough.\n\nValidation: a new boundary test pipes install.sh to `bash -s --\n--dry-run` truncated at every byte offset from the end of main() to EOF\nand asserts that npm and the rea binary are never invoked; offsets\ninside the group must exit non-zero with a bash parse error and no\nstdout, offsets before it must print nothing, and the complete group\nmust finish the dry run. Reproduced the original bug by cutting the old\nscript after `main`: the dry run invoked npm install. `bash -n` and\nshellcheck 0.11.0 are clean.\n\n* fix(install): wrap the whole script in the truncation guard\n\nThe previous guard only wrapped the final `main \"$@\"` invocation, so the\npreamble streamed on the `curl ... | bash` path before any incomplete\ncompound command was open. A download cut off right after the bytes\n`set` reached EOF with a complete, argument-less `set`, which prints every\nshell variable, so inherited tokens could reach stdout.\n\nThe brace group now opens on line 2, directly after the shebang, and\ncloses on the last line, enclosing `set -euo pipefail`, the constants, the\nfunctions and the final `main \"$@\"` / `exit $?`. Bash must therefore read\nthe whole file before running anything; any earlier cut is a syntax error\nat EOF. `set -euo pipefail` still applies inside the group (checked\nmanually for `false`, an unbound variable, and a failing function body),\nand shellcheck 0.11.0 and `bash -n` are clean.\n\nThe truncation test now pipes every prefix of install.sh, from byte 0 to\nEOF (about 6,100 cuts, run 16 at a time with a minimal environment that\nincludes a sentinel variable), into `bash -s -- --dry-run`. Before the\ngroup opens it must exit quietly; inside the group it must fail with a\nsyntax error; in every case stdout stays empty and neither npm nor rea is\ninvoked. Only the complete script performs the dry run.",
