    {
      "sha": "4b653fe6ad1d25a2d9a69d5e93610380c0c09a34",
      "subject": "fix(native): accept universal binaries with LIB64 x86_64 slices (#1982)",
      "body": "fix(native): accept universal binaries with LIB64 x86_64 slices (#1982)\n\n* fix(native): apply printed lipo capability bits to CPU subtypes\n\nlipo -detailed_info prints a slice's capability byte separately from its\nsymbolic subtype: CPU_SUBTYPE_LIB64 for x86_64 and ppc64, and other bits\nas hex. Only the arm64e pointer-auth form was applied, so a LIB64 x86_64\nslice normalized to subtype 3 while its FAT entry carries 0x80000003,\nand slice validation rejected the universal binary as an integrity\nmismatch. Fold the printed capability byte into the subtype code.\n\n* fix(native): leave lipo CPU subtypes unknown without capability bits\n\nThe lipo shipped with macOS 26 prints an empty capabilities line for\nx86_64 slices whose FAT capability byte is 0x80 as well as 0x00, so a\nsymbolic subtype alone does not identify the slice's CPU subtype. The\nparser assumed no capability bits and still reported subtype 3 for\nLIB64 slices, which made universal applications such as Sublime Text\nfail slice validation. Report the subtype code as unknown when lipo\nprints no capability byte, as arm64e already does; slice validation\nthen relies on the remaining physical facts.\n\n* test(native): cover printed and blank lipo capability bits\n\nFold CPU_SUBTYPE_LIB64 and hex capability bytes into x86_64 subtype\ncodes, and keep the code unknown for the blank capabilities line that\nmacOS 26 lipo prints.\n\n* test(native): match a LIB64 x86_64 slice with blank lipo capabilities\n\nRead a universal image whose x86_64 FAT entry carries 0x80000003 while\nlipo reports CPU_SUBTYPE_X86_ALL with a blank capabilities line, as\nmacOS 26 lipo does, and expect both slices instead of an integrity\nfailure.\n\n* fix(native): compare named lipo subtype bits without capability bytes\n\nA blank capabilities line leaves the subtype code unknown, and slice\nmatching then skipped the subtype entirely. A FAT entry whose named\nsubtype differs, such as CPU_SUBTYPE_ARM64_V8 recorded by lipo as\nCPU_SUBTYPE_ARM64_ALL, was accepted. Keep comparing the complete\nsubtype when lipo printed its capability byte, and otherwise compare\nthe subtype bits its record names.\n\n* test(native): share LIB64 slice reader setup\n\nRead the LIB64 universal fixture through one helper that takes the\nx86_64 subtype lipo reports, so further cases reuse the same image and\nlipo record. The existing expectation is unchanged.\n\n* test(native): reject a blank-capability lipo subtype naming other bits\n\nWith the capabilities line blank, a lipo record naming\nCPU_SUBTYPE_X86_64_H for the x86_64 LIB64 slice still disagrees with\nthe FAT entry's subtype bits and fails slice validation.",
      "parents": ["0d11f71e0dc5cf95d3bea093355f7edc09089a68"],
      "authors": [
