      "sha": "a1b5d51a8dfb7fff6272d91270c16b95555c2643",
      "subject": "feat(go): inspect embedded binary build metadata (#1363)",
      "body": "feat(go): inspect embedded binary build metadata (#1363)\n\n* feat(go): inspect embedded binary build metadata\n\n* fix(go): preserve byte metadata and validate container mappings\n\n* fix(go): support moduleless legacy build metadata\n\nRecognize optional module string headers in declared zero-filled memory while rejecting ambiguous and unmapped pointers. Preserve the original module-pointer field as the zero-byte source anchor.\n\nClassify initial symlink-loop and overlong-path failures as invalid input, with CLI and MCP regressions.\n\n* fix(go): reject undersized PE optional headers\n\n* fix(native): stabilize ELF memory checks and clean up exec wrappers\n\nUse existing parent/group/run-token ownership for caller-selected Python launchers, which can exec through a shell or prlimit. Add cancellation regressions for the valid wrapper and wrong-parent/token cases.\n\nReplace the timing-dependent 30,000-section memory workload with a valid 32 MiB padded ELF under a 64 MiB address-space limit. Check the same image successfully through CLI and MCP before requiring a typed memory failure, preserving the production timeout and all error assertions.\n\nValidation: npm run check and check:fast passed; 61 focused tests passed including Go CLI/MCP and session snapshots. Real decoder probes with pinned packages passed on Python 3.12.3 and 3.12.14. The full local ELF verifier is blocked by this container's procps/process-ownership boundary; the exact pushed head requires GitHub's real-binary-layout lane.\n\n* test: repair Electron fixtures and stabilize catalog startup checks\n\nDeclare Electron imports in option, preload override, and path-resolution fixtures after upstream began requiring proven API provenance. Retain all option and preload assertions and adjust exact source ranges for the added import line.\n\nUse the existing bounded acceptance timeout for the cold catalog lifecycle test instead of a separate 15-second ceiling. This retains the coverage allowance and all catalog, error, and cleanup assertions without retries.\n\nValidation: npm run check, final check:fast, formatting and diff checks passed. Source path regression and Go boundary/MCP/service checks passed. All 20 Go CLI cases and both supported-host runtime cases passed on rerun; two macOS-only cases skipped on Linux. A prior contended local run timed out once in a Go CLI diagnostic; that case passed on rerun. Electron process-boundary verification is blocked locally by procps namespace inspection and requires fresh CI.\n\n* test(mcp): isolate nested schema references between tool contracts\n\nClear Ajv's non-meta schema registry after each root, including compilation failures. Disabling root registration alone leaves nested IDs available to later tools and can let a dangling reference pass the catalog gate.\n\nAdd regressions for leakage after successful and failed compilation while retaining shared meta-schema validators. The regression failed before the fix; all 11 schema cases and full npm run check pass. Reviewed independently.\n\n* test(ci): align Go effects and source-map verification contracts\n\nAudit the Go inspector wording as static metadata inspection. Assert retained source declaration indexes in BOM reconstruction and the compiler-derived nested-map oracle. Keep a 45,000-map admission control below the 32 MiB output budget and the 50,000-map overflow case above it with the expanded representation.\n\nValidation: reproduced all four failing assertions, then passed 120 focused tests and the final admission suite. npm run check and real Chrome 154 / esbuild 0.25.10 source-map verification passed, including all four nested-map scenarios and verified process lineage.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
