      "sha": "69e097a8ca256ab97ee4b2883739447784a5f90e",
      "subject": "fix(scripts, install): honor test:fast \"without build\" promise and orient Node.js engine mismatches (#2026)",
      "body": "fix(scripts, install): honor test:fast \"without build\" promise and orient Node.js engine mismatches (#2026)\n\n* fix(scripts): defer dist/ imports in verifier helpers so test:fast runs without build\n\nTwo helpers under scripts/lib/ reached into ../../dist/ at module load even\nthough the top-level import was only needed by one of their exports. That\nturned mocked, dist-free tests into failing tests under `npm run test:fast`,\nwhich docs/testing.md § test scripts explicitly promises runs \"without build\":\n\n- tests/conformance/providers/hopper/realHopperSemantic.test.ts only calls\n  requireCurrentDocument and resolveFixtureProcedure (both mock-only), but\n  scripts/lib/real-hopper-semantic.mjs eagerly imported\n  requireFunctionDossierOracle from dist/application/RealHopperAssertions.js.\n  Moved the dist-dependent dossier oracle into a lazy loader called from\n  verifyRealHopperFixture, which is where it is actually used (against the\n  real Hopper bridge, after build:cached).\n\n- tests/evaluation/package/packagePlatformDiagnostics.test.ts only uses\n  assertLinuxPackageProviderFailure, which depends on `json`, but\n  scripts/lib/verify-package-core.mjs eagerly re-exported helpers from\n  mcp-verifier-results.mjs, which in turn pulls four dist/ modules. Moved\n  that import into verifyCompleteToolCatalog (the only consumer in this\n  file). Downstream scripts (verify-recorded-crash.mjs, verify-package-mcp.mjs,\n  verify-windows-package.mjs) are unaffected because they already run after\n  build:cached.\n\nReproduction before fix:\n  rm -rf dist && npm run test:fast → 2 failed (both import-time errors)\n\nReproduction after fix:\n  rm -rf dist && npm run test:fast → 388 passed, 4924 tests, 0 failed\n  npm run build:cached && npm run test:fast → 388 passed, 4924 tests, 0 failed\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(install): orient Node.js version mismatch failures with actionable remediation\n\ninstall.sh previously told operators \"use Node.js 22.x (>=22.19), 24.x (>=24.11),\nor 26+\" without saying how to install one. The curl|bash onboarding path then\nleft the operator to guess between nvm, fnm, n, Volta, system packages, or\nnodejs.org — a lookup that bounces across search engines for a first-time user.\n\nReplace the one-line failure with a block that:\n- Names the detected Node.js version (so the operator confirms it is theirs).\n- Shows the three most common install paths (nvm, fnm, direct tarball).\n- Pins the Node.js release line (24) that satisfies the engine, so copy-paste\n  commands produce a supported runtime instead of whichever \"latest\" is on top.\n- Maps uname -m / uname -s to the Node.js tarball label pair for this host\n  (e.g. darwin/arm64, linux/x64), so the operator can grab the right archive\n  from https://nodejs.org/dist/latest-v24.x/ without a second lookup.\n\nVerified with four Node.js versions (24.18 compatible, 25.9/20.11 outside the\nsupported ranges, 22.18 below the minor floor) that remediation triggers only\non real mismatches and names the host labels correctly. shellcheck install.sh\nand npm run check:fast remain clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
