    {
      "sha": "e4d3e1399d9cd4932653e7015a1bf8589e56029d",
      "subject": "feat(flutter): identify Dart snapshot builds and inspect AOT payloads by pure APK parsing (#1582)",
      "body": "feat(flutter): identify Dart snapshot builds and inspect AOT payloads by pure APK parsing (#1582)\n\n* feat(flutter): identify Dart snapshot builds by pure APK parsing\n\nAdd a flutter-provider family with one operation, identify_flutter_build,\nthat reads an APK's lib/<abi>/libapp.so and libflutter.so through the\nhardened ZIP reader and reports build facts as observations:\n\n- The Dart VM snapshot hash, read 20 bytes after every snapshot magic\n  (f5f5dcdc). The vm and isolate sections are cross-checked: agreeing\n  sections collapse to one snapshot_hash, disagreeing sections keep every\n  candidate with snapshot_hash null and partial coverage.\n- Per-ABI library digests and byte sizes, plus the engine library's GNU\n  build-id note and any labeled 'Dart VM version' or Android clang/LLVM\n  toolchain strings, verbatim; release builds without a version string\n  report null rather than a guess.\n- The APK is digested before parsing so Evidence binds the observation to\n  the exact input, and a target with no libapp.so reports not-detected\n  with complete coverage.\n\nThe scanner is streaming with explicit pending-magic and rescan\nbookkeeping, so chunk boundaries cannot double-count or drop sections, and\nmagic-free streams never accumulate. No engine, no external lookups: the\nhash-to-SDK mapping stays with external datasets by design.\n\nVerified against Flutter Gallery 2.9.2 (112 MB, three ABIs): every ABI\nreports the same agreeing hash 65817c30a78bb44c3dc3771876b6010a from two\nsections, per-ABI build-ids, and the clang toolchain line; a plain\naapt2-built APK reports not-detected with complete coverage. Scanner\ngoldens come from the same libraries. READMEs carry the new 'Flutter apps'\nrow in every supported language.\n\n* feat(flutter): inspect Dart AOT snapshots through the ELF symbol table\n\nAdd inspect_dart_aot to the flutter family: for one ABI's libapp.so it\nlocates the four _kDart*Snapshot* symbols through the ELF dynamic symbol\ntable (ELF32 and ELF64 little-endian, bounds-checked reads; a payload\nwithout exported symbols is refused), reports each section's offset and\nsize, validates data-section headers (magic, kind, length; instructions\nsections honestly report nulls for the fields their layout does not\ncarry), and projects a bounded string pool from the isolate data section:\ndistinct package: source URIs (the app's dependency inventory), dart: SDK\nURIs, and identifier-shaped tokens, each with counts, bounds, and partial\ncoverage when truncated.\n\nThe string pool is labeled as a byte-pattern projection, not deserialized\ncluster semantics: typed class and function recovery remains an\nSDK-specific parser, which the snapshot hash now unlocks. The ABI is\ncaller-selected with a deterministic default (arm64-v8a, x86_64, then\nfirst available).\n\nVerified against Flutter Gallery 2.9.2: all four symbols on both ELF64\n(arm64-v8a) and ELF32 (armeabi-v7a) images, header validation, hash\nagreement with identify_flutter_build, and a 756 package: / 201 dart:\nURI pool from the isolate data section; goldens in DartElf.test.ts come\nfrom the same libraries.\n\n* fix(flutter): own snapshots, bound image reads, and preserve cancellation\n\nMirror the ownership bar the maintainers applied across the merged JEB,\nADB, and Apktool PRs:\n\n- Copy the admitted APK into a provider-owned, read-only snapshot (EXCL\n  create, chmod 0400) before digesting and parsing; every later read uses\n  the snapshot, so a concurrently modified original cannot mix into one\n  observation. The result reports the snapshot's byte size and digest.\n- Retain failed snapshot-root cleanup in a provider set and retry it on\n  close(), throwing an aggregate error when a root is still owned; root\n  removal is injectable so the retention is testable.\n- Allocate the AOT image buffer at exactly the declared library size\n  before reading (bounded before allocation, not concat-after-stream),\n  keep the 256 MiB ceiling, and check cancellation between digest and\n  stream chunks through a captured abort reason.\n- Accept APK paths containing spaces (covered by a fixture test) and\n  escape the ABI pattern's dash for v-mode regex compatibility.\n\nAlso add tests for cleanup retention with close retry and for\ncancellation before any snapshot root is created.\n\n* docs(flutter): cover the Italian README with the Flutter row\n\nThe Italian translation landed between the merged feature PRs; its table\nnow carries every merged row plus this one, keeping its documentation-link\nset identical to README.md.\n\n* fix(flutter): make the ABI pattern portable for v-mode validators\n\nUse a hex-escaped hyphen in the ABI character class, matching the form\nthe ADB portability fix uses, so clients compiling advertised schemas in\nunicodeSets mode accept the inspect_dart_aot input schema.\n\n* refactor(flutter): reuse shared harnesses and deduplicate the vertical\n\nThe duplicate-detection gate (jscpd, 2.5% threshold) tipped to 2.51% with\nthe flutter files added. Reuse the existing machinery instead of repeating\nit:\n\n- FlutterBuildAnalysisService delegates to executeProviderOperation, the\n  shared admission/Evidence composition, instead of restating its body.\n- The CLI registers both commands from one table-driven spec.\n- Boundary tests use the shared serveCli and assertAdvertisedMcpContracts\n  harnesses rather than hand-rolled equivalents.\n- The provider test consolidates the four payload-shape cases into one\n  table-driven case; the service test uses one admission table and\n  distinct phrasings.\n\nDuplicate lines fall from 2.51% to 2.48%, back under the gate.\n\n* fix(flutter): address review findings from real-app verification\n\nCassieFox23 verified the PR against three real F-Droid Flutter apps and\na control app, and found five gaps. All are fixed and re-verified against\ncone (info.tangential.cone), one of the tested apps:\n\n- Build-id: the scanner now reads the descriptor length from the note\n  header instead of assuming 20 bytes, so 8-byte ids from older\n  toolchains parse; the schema accepts 8-128 hex characters. Verified\n  against cone's readelf-reported ids on all three ABIs.\n- Compiler line: the toolchain capture bound rises from 200 to 500\n  characters, keeping cone's 262-character clang line verbatim.\n- Zero-byte libapp.so: the extraction target is created before streaming,\n  so an empty entry yields a clean unsupported_target refusal for\n  inspect_dart_aot and elf_header_valid: false with partial coverage for\n  identify_flutter_build, instead of a raw ENOENT with code UNKNOWN.\n- Truncated library: identify_flutter_build marks fewer than two snapshot\n  sections as partial coverage with a limitation naming the count, and a\n  missing ELF magic gets its own limitation.\n- Docs: the default ABI preference now matches the code exactly\n  (arm64-v8a, x86_64, x86, armeabi-v7a, then the rest alphabetically).\n\nAlso: every test that read the author's /tmp/opencode goldens now builds\na self-contained synthetic ELF64 image with dynsym, four snapshot\nsymbols, two snapshot sections, and a string pool, so the suite runs\nanywhere; the verify lane now requires a present hex build-id per ABI\ninstead of accepting null.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the Codex review findings\n\nEight findings, all applied:\n\n- P1: SnapshotHashScanner keeps candidates in a Set (no quadratic\n  Array.includes) with a 64-distinct-hash cap, so a hostile payload of\n  magic+hash records cannot stall the scan or bloat the response.\n- P1: projectStringPool stops admitting new distinct entries once each\n  category reaches twice its projection bound and reports\n  collectionCapped; allocation and sorting now track the response, not\n  the payload.\n- P2: ZIP reader rejections during entry iteration or entry streaming\n  map into the typed error algebra (cancellation vs input refusal)\n  instead of escaping as unhandled exceptions in both operations.\n- P2: magic_valid now marks coverage only for the *Data symbols; a\n  complete four-symbol image with valid data magics reports complete\n  coverage (the instruction symbols intentionally lack the magic).\n- P2: the MCP server closes the Flutter provider during shutdown, so a\n  retained snapshot root is retried and reported, not leaked.\n- P2: LabeledStringScanner commits matches only when a printable run is\n  delimiter-terminated; the carry grows to 600 bytes so a 500-character\n  toolchain line survives any chunk boundary untruncated.\n- P2: both Flutter tool effects declare writesFilesystem: true; the\n  owned APK snapshot and extracted libapp.so are temporary writes.\n- P2: the verify lane asserts every ABI agrees on exactly one snapshot\n  hash instead of discarding the computed set.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the second Codex review round\n\nNine findings on the first-round fixes:\n\n- P1: identify_flutter_build enforces a cumulative extraction budget\n  (768 MiB) across every library entry, not just per library, so an APK\n  with many ABIs cannot stream an unbounded total.\n- P1: a single NUL-free printable run stops materializing at 1024 bytes\n  and marks the pool capped, so an adversarial one-run section cannot\n  allocate unbounded strings.\n- P1: ELF cString bounds the NUL-terminator scan to the name maximum\n  plus one, so malformed symbol tables cannot scan the whole image per\n  entry.\n- P2: mapReaderFailure distinguishes ArtifactReaderFailure texts —\n  budget/limit rejections map to resource constraints, integrity\n  failures (CRC/overlap/signature) to their own input refusal — instead\n  of collapsing all reader failures into one message.\n- P2: the labeled-string carry is cleared when a delimiter commits a\n  run, so consecutive toolchain lines are never concatenated.\n- P2: string-pool counts are renamed *_count_at_least and documented as\n  lower bounds, because capped collection discards values beyond twice\n  the projection bound.\n- P2: inspect_dart_aot marks hash disagreements, non-pair source counts,\n  and candidate-cap saturation as partial coverage.\n- P2: only valid lowercase-hex hash records count as snapshot sources;\n  complete magic records with non-hex fields do not inflate agreement.\n- P2: the per-entry size refusal in inspect_dart_aot moves after ABI\n  selection, so an oversized unselected ABI no longer refuses a small\n  request.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the third Codex review round\n\nFive findings:\n\n- P2: the owned snapshot revalidates the source after the copy (size,\n  mtime, inode), so an in-place replacement or swap mid-copy refuses\n  the observation instead of publishing mixed bytes.\n- P2: a failed ZipArtifactReader close retains the snapshot root for\n  close() retry instead of silently swallowing the descriptor leak,\n  in both operations.\n- P2: snapshot headers are parsed within their symbol bounds and a\n  symbol smaller than the 16-byte header reports magic_valid false,\n  so trailing-image bytes can no longer fake a valid data section.\n- P2: the verify lane counts provider.close() cleanup failures as\n  lane failures instead of certifying a run with retained roots.\n- P2: LabeledStringScanner reports toolchain_capped when further\n  distinct matching lines are observed past the 4-line projection;\n  identify marks such results partial with a naming limitation.\n\n* fix(flutter): address rounds three and four of the Codex review\n\nRound three (one missed finding) plus round four (five findings):\n\n- P2: the GNU build-id note descriptor size is decoded from all four\n  bytes of the little-endian field, rejecting nonzero high bytes\n  instead of masking them to 16 bits.\n- P1: the Flutter MCP handlers route through createContractToolHandler\n  and the session's analysisAdmission, so close_binary and snapshot\n  operations drain in-flight Flutter calls instead of racing them.\n- P2: the owned-snapshot copy is a streamed, abort-checked write (no\n  copyFile), so cancellation stops the write promptly while the\n  source-identity revalidation (size, mtime, inode) still guards\n  against mid-copy replacement.\n- P2: identify marks any snapshot_hash_sources value other than exactly\n  two as partial, matching the inspection path's pair requirement.\n- P2: the verify lane refuses to run without --apk (exit 2) instead of\n  printing a false-green pass, and the engine check requires every\n  libapp-carrying ABI to have a present libflutter with a valid hex\n  build-id rather than filtering missing engines away vacuously.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the fifth Codex review round\n\nSix findings plus the upstream duplication baseline:\n\n- P2: the flutter-provider kind reports available in the capability\n  inventory, so binary_session no longer lists always-registered\n  Flutter tools as provider_missing.\n- P2: printable runs that exceed the 1024-byte cap are discarded at\n  their delimiter instead of emitting their truncated prefix as a\n  fabricated observation.\n- P2: ELF symbols must fit inside the file-backed portion of their\n  PT_LOAD segment (p_filesz), so addresses in gaps or BSS are refused\n  instead of accepting unrelated bytes.\n- P2: the build-id carry spans the complete 80-byte maximum note\n  (16-byte header + 64-byte descriptor) so chunk-boundary splits\n  cannot drop the first byte.\n- P2: the labeled-string carry is sized for the toolchain pattern's\n  true 605-byte maximum, not the previous 600.\n- P2: the AOT completeness predicate requires exactly two sources and\n  one candidate unconditionally, so zero-source images with valid data\n  magics report partial instead of complete.\n\nAlso: the shared ELF fixture builder and snapshot-section helper moved\nto a dedicated FlutterTestFixtures module, eliminating cross-file test\nduplication; jscpd now passes at 2.50% while main sits at 2.52%.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the sixth Codex review round\n\nFive findings:\n\n- P2: snapshot-hash scanning is bounded to the exported data symbols'\n  byte ranges, so decoy or stale magic/hash sequences elsewhere in the\n  image cannot inflate source counts or contribute candidates.\n- P2: labeled-string runs longer than the carry bound are rejected\n  instead of truncated, preventing a regex prefix from being published\n  as a verbatim source string.\n- P2: the AOT image reread checks cancellation between file reads and\n  before the synchronous parsing stage, so a cancelled caller cannot\n  receive a success from a completed 256 MiB read.\n- P2: the verify lane requires exactly two snapshot sources per ABI,\n  matching the provider's pair requirement, instead of accepting any\n  count >= 2.\n- P2: ELF section and program header entry sizes are validated against\n  the class-defined minimum widths before walking either table, so\n  undersized entries cannot cause field reads to cross into\n  neighboring bytes.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the seventh Codex review round\n\nSix findings:\n\n- P2: data-symbol ranges are deduplicated by offset and size, so aliased\n  symbols mapping to the same bytes are scanned once and the result\n  marks partial coverage with a limitation naming the shared range.\n- P2: openEntrySafely forwards return/throw to the underlying Readable's\n  iterator, so an early for-await exit closes the ZIP entry stream and\n  releases its producer instead of leaking a backpressured pipe.\n- P2: inspect_dart_aot now reports snapshot_hash_candidates and\n  snapshot_hash_sources alongside snapshot_hash, so a null hash from\n  conflicting evidence is distinguishable from missing hash data.\n- P2: overlong printable runs only set toolchain_capped when they begin\n  with a recognized label prefix (Dart VM version: or Android (); other\n  long strings such as license text are skipped without marking partial.\n- P2: readElfSymbol falls back to PT_DYNAMIC/DT_SYMTAB when section\n  headers are absent (e_shnum 0, ET_DYN), so stripped Flutter libraries\n  with exported snapshot symbols are no longer refused; the dynamic\n  table is bounded by DT_STRSZ, DT_SYMENT, and the image end.\n- P2: the verify lane exercises inspect_dart_aot for every ABI carrying\n  libapp.so, not just the preferred default.\n\n* fix(flutter): address the eighth Codex review round\n\nFive findings:\n\n- P2: each data-symbol range gets a fresh SnapshotHashScanner so the\n  carry from one noncontiguous slice cannot fabricate a record spanning\n  into the next; results are aggregated after scanning.\n- P2: dynsym name lookups are bounded to the linked string table —\n  st_name offsets outside the table and terminators beyond it are\n  rejected instead of reading unrelated payload bytes as symbol names,\n  in both the section-header and PT_DYNAMIC paths.\n- P2: identify_flutter_build routes its reader.entries and reader.open\n  calls through the same collectEntriesSafely and openEntrySafely\n  wrappers as inspect_dart_aot, so malformed ZIPs surface as input\n  refusals instead of AnalysisProtocolError.\n- P2: mapReaderFailure recognizes ArtifactReaderFailure texts carrying\n  a cancelled reason, mapping them to AnalysisCancelledError instead\n  of AnalysisInputError.\n- P2: the cumulative extraction cap is enforced inside the chunk loop,\n  not after each library completes, so refusal happens before the\n  remaining bytes are streamed and hashed.\n\n* fix(flutter): harden streaming, sizes, and cancellation after self-review\n\nFour issues found in a systematic self-audit across all bug classes\nfrom eight Codex rounds:\n\n- createReadStream in both the digest function and the owned-snapshot\n  copy now receives the AbortSignal directly, so cancellation aborts\n  the filesystem read at the source instead of waiting for the next\n  chunk to arrive on a slow filesystem.\n- The owned snapshot enforces a 2 GiB APK-level size cap before any\n  copy I/O, so a path pointing at an enormous file is refused before\n  disk usage grows.\n- identify_flutter_build preflights the sum of declared entry sizes\n  against the 768 MiB cumulative budget, refusing before any ZIP\n  extraction when the declared total already exceeds it.\n- The LabeledStringScanner triple result() call is collapsed to a\n  single invocation (the previous code called result() three times in\n  the emission, relying on end() being idempotent).\n\nNo behavior change for valid inputs; all 36 tests, check:fast, and\nthe cone verify lane pass unchanged.\n\n* fix(flutter): require structurally parseable ELF headers for complete coverage\n\nCassieFox23's third corrupt-header case: an ELF with valid magic but\nzeroed section/program header offsets passes the 4-byte magic check\nand the byte scanner still finds hash patterns in the data sections,\nso identify_flutter_build reports complete coverage for a structurally\nbroken file.\n\nThe elf_header_valid check now also verifies beyond the magic:\ne_type must be a known value (ET_REL/ET_EXEC/ET_DYN), at least one\nof e_shoff/e_phoff must be non-zero, and at least one of\ne_shnum/e_phnum must be non-zero. A file with valid magic but zeroed\nstructure reports partial coverage with the existing ELF-magic\nlimitation.\n\n* fix(flutter): validate ELF and snapshot coverage\n\nShare bounded ELF header parsing between identification and inspection. Accumulate headers across ZIP chunks and validate table entry widths and extents before claiming complete identification coverage.\n\nBind AOT hashes to the exported VM and isolate headers, validate stored snapshot lengths including the magic bytes, and preserve partial evidence for missing, malformed, aliased, or overlapping data headers.\n\nPreserve native stream cancellations and APK resource constraints across both operations. Bound snapshot copies while streaming and retain useful input-failure diagnostics and lower-bound count wording.\n\nValidation: 72 focused tests; check:fast; check including formatting; docs:check; test discovery; jscpd; real cone APK and non-Flutter control on Linux. Verified CLI/MCP parity for valid and corrupted ELF headers and AOT inspection across three ABIs. Native Windows and macOS were not run.\n\n* fix(flutter): unify artifact ownership and bound ELF evidence\n\n* fix(flutter): preserve ELF provenance and partial coverage\n\nRead GNU build IDs only from bounded ELF note ranges and retain conflicting candidates. Preserve source versus owned storage failures and honor cancellation through parsing and final cleanup.\n\nSeparate snapshot structure from string-pool coverage. Verify every Gallery ABI with digest-bound engine labels and URI counts, and document the supported snapshot-kind layout.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: REA Contributor <contributor@rea.tools>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
