      "sha": "0baad58e606f12a8dc6cef06253f85350378e5b1",
      "subject": "feat(ghidra): admit RISC-V ELF analysis through a pinned generic lane (#2151)",
      "body": "feat(ghidra): admit RISC-V ELF analysis through a pinned generic lane (#2151)\n\n* feat(ghidra): admit RISC-V ELF analysis through a pinned generic lane\n\nRefs #2063.\n\nRecognize `EM_RISCV` (243) as a provider-neutral family that carries its own ELF\nfacts, and bound the first Ghidra lane to little-endian `ET_EXEC` images that\ndeclare the double-float ABI, pinned to `RISCV:LE:32:default` or\n`RISCV:LE:64:default` with the `gcc` compiler specification.\n\nGhidra 12.1.4 is not reliable without the pin: its `riscv.opinion` pins\n`variant=\"default\"` for the 64-bit constraint and leaves the 32-bit constraint\nwith the comment that the loader \"must choose between 'default' and 'AndeStar'\nvariants\". Measured on the installed release tree, a source-owned generic RV32\nELF with no Andes declaration imports as `RISCV:LE:32:AndeStar_v5:gcc`, while\nRV64 resolves to `RISCV:LE:64:default:gcc`. The lane therefore states the\nlanguage per width and refuses any caller-selected processor outside it instead\nof inheriting the header guess.\n\nRefusals name the declaration rather than falling back to a guessed profile:\nbig-endian images (Ghidra 12.1.4 ships no big-endian RISC-V language),\nnon-`ET_EXEC` images, `EF_RISCV_RVE`, `EF_RISCV_TSO`, and every float ABI other\nthan double, which the pinned generic compiler specifications model. The raw\ne_flags bits stay in Evidence as declarations, not as instruction semantics.\n\nFamily recognition now has one owner for the ELF-metadata families:\n`ELF_METADATA_ARCHITECTURES`, `isElfMetadataArchitecture`, and\n`HeaderArchitecture` replace ten `Exclude<BinaryArchitecture, \"mips\">` sites that\nwould each have needed a second hand-edited member. `ghidraPinnedLanguage` is\nthat owner for the import commitment: the session readback previously re-derived\nonly the MIPS pin, so a profile committing any second pinned language failed with\na language-mismatch error before the bridge started.\n\nHopper and IDA refuse RISC-V by name, matching the MIPS wording. Caller-supplied\nraw-binary entry keeps its existing four-family list; this lane is ELF-header\nadmission only.\n\nVerification on macOS 15.7.7 arm64, Node 24.18.0, Ghidra 12.1.4 PUBLIC and JDK\n21.0.12.1, with the host decompiler built from the release sources for\n`os/mac_arm_64`: `npm run check` passes; `npm run test:fast` passes 396 test\nfiles; `npm run verify:test-discovery` discovers all 917 test files exactly once.\nSource-owned RV32 and RV64 double-float `ET_EXEC` fixtures complete\n`rea analyze --provider ghidra` and `rea decompile rea_riscv_entry`, committing\n`RISCV:LE:32:default` and `RISCV:LE:64:default` with `language_selection:\nprovider-pinned-v1`; the soft-float RV64 shape is refused with the named\nfloat-ABI reason, and the full refusal matrix including the RV32 and\nwidth-mismatch shapes is covered by unit tests. No `verify:ghidra:riscv` lane\nscript is included in this change; the runs above were performed manually against\nthe installed engine. Fork pull requests receive no CI check runs, so the\nreal-provider scope above is the evidence available for review.\n\n* fix(ghidra): bound the RISC-V lane by declared flags and document it\n\nAddress review feedback on #2151.\n\n`e_flags` bits outside `EF_RISCV_RVC | EF_RISCV_FLOAT_ABI` are now refused with\nthe offending value named, so a reserved or newer producer declaration cannot\nenter the verified generic lane by falling through the RVE, TSO and float-ABI\nchecks.\n\nAdmission reads `e_type` only, so the refusal text no longer calls `ET_EXEC`\nstatic: a fixed-address declaration does not establish static linkage, and\n`PT_INTERP` and dynamic dependencies are not inspected. The session records that\nboundary as a limitation rather than inferring a load model.\n\nAdd `docs/ghidra-riscv.md` in the shape of the MIPS lane guide (admission\nboundary, refusal table, why the language is pinned per class, Evidence fields,\nand what the fixtures do not establish), and correct\n`docs/firmware-analysis.md`, which still told users that MIPS and RISC-V\ndecompilation had not been added.\n\nVerification after the change: `npm run check` and `npm run docs:check` pass; the\nRISC-V module tests pass (17 lane cases plus 9 parser cases); on Ghidra 12.1.4\nwith JDK 21.0.12.1 the RV32 and RV64 double-float fixtures still analyze and\ndecompile with `RISCV:LE:32:default` / `RISCV:LE:64:default` and\n`language_selection: provider-pinned-v1`, the soft-float fixture is still refused\nby name, and the new linkage limitation appears in the emitted Evidence.\n\n* fix(ghidra): align RISC-V pinning with admission\n\nReuse the complete lane admission boundary when selecting a language, retain unsigned unsupported flag bits in diagnostics, and derive float ABI metadata from the source declaration.\n\n* test(ghidra): verify RISC-V CLI and MCP workflows\n\nBuild source-owned RV32/RV64 fixtures with Clang and LLD, establish independent ELF and symbol facts with LLVM readelf, and verify real provider evidence, instruction bytes, calls, decompilation, schema projections, snapshot replay, refusal diagnostics, source immutability and cleanup.\n\nVerified on macOS ARM64 with Ghidra 12.1.4, JDK 21 and LLVM/LLD 23.1.2. Dynamic linkage, big-endian, Andes and RVE/TSO workflows remain unverified; ISA attributes are not inspected.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
