    {
      "sha": "7b15762d09acf3d4ffb937f55ec1b54e7db5fbfa",
      "subject": "fix(reference): report sloppy scripts, PHP, C# and enclosing Git facts (#1770)",
      "body": "fix(reference): report sloppy scripts, PHP, C# and enclosing Git facts (#1770)\n\n* fix(reference): report sloppy scripts, PHP, C# and enclosing Git facts\n\nImporting the source/ subtree of a legacy PHP and C# checkout produced\nfour wrong or missing facts:\n\n- Babel's unambiguous mode parses a module first and keeps its\n  strict-mode recovery errors after falling back to a script, so a valid\n  sloppy-mode `delete messages;` was reported as a parse failure and the\n  semantic IR marked the file as parser-recovered. Parsing now goes\n  through one owner that re-parses such a script as a script.\n- .php and .cs files had no language; they are now detected as PHP and\n  C# (detection only, no parsing).\n- A root without its own .git was reported as having no version\n  control even inside a checkout. The adapter now resolves the nearest\n  enclosing repository, as Git does, and reports `none` only when no\n  ancestor has one.\n- The CLI recorded importer_version as null although the package\n  version is known.\n\n* fix(reference): keep module strictness and classify C# and PHP as source\n\nReview follow-up. `.mjs` and `.mts` files are modules by extension, but\nthe script fallback re-parsed them in sloppy mode and erased real\nstrict-mode errors. The parser boundary now owns the extension rule,\nparses explicit modules as modules and applies the fallback only when\nthe mode is ambiguous; the require-binding analysis uses the same rule.\n\n`.cs` and `.php` received a language but stayed classified as unknown,\nso source-to-bundle comparison dropped them. Both are now source\nextensions, like the other detected languages.\n\n* fix(reference): honor package module scope and keep unknown-mode strict errors\n\nReview follow-up. A `.js` file under a package.json with `\"type\":\n\"module\"` is an ES module, but it went through the script fallback that\nerases strict-mode errors. The import now reads each in-inventory\npackage.json and parses `.js` files in a module scope as modules. Other\n`.js` and `.ts` sources keep an unknown mode, because bundlers treat them\nas modules without that field: they are no longer forced to script, and\nerrors that only the module parse reports are kept as an entry limitation.\n\n* fix(reference): bound package scopes and keep application parsing unchanged\n\nReview follow-up.\n- `.cjs` files parse as scripts, so module-only strict errors no longer\n  become limitations on CommonJS trees. `.cts` keeps an unknown mode\n  because TypeScript compiles ES import syntax there.\n- An unreadable or linked package.json now bounds the module scope with\n  an unknown type instead of letting an outer `\"type\": \"module\"` apply.\n- The application pipeline parses as on main again. Its module-only\n  strict errors keep a source partial; package-mode handling stays in the\n  reference importer.\n\n* refactor(reference): keep unmarked module modes unknown instead of resolving package scope\n\nReview follow-up. Resolving Node package scope for `.js` kept pulling in\nmore of Node's lookup semantics (require bindings, excluded or\ndirectory-named manifests). Only extensions now select a mode: `.mjs`\nand `.mts` parse as modules and `.cjs` as scripts. Every other source\nkeeps an unknown mode, so module-only strict errors stay visible as an\nentry limitation instead of being dropped or asserted. This matches the\nrequire-binding analysis, which also treats unmarked paths as unknown.",
      "parents": ["3ee6ff43c1696a5a5269bd4aa5de72f7e5f40627"],
      "authors": [
