      "sha": "d59ab6d977d5b9376af2fdd141b4f65460bb9a9f",
      "subject": "fix(javascript): bound value evaluation through nested closure aliases (#1941)",
      "body": "fix(javascript): bound value evaluation through nested closure aliases (#1941)\n\n* fix(javascript): bound value evaluation through nested closure aliases\n\nA closure that aliases a binding from its enclosing scope evaluates that\nbinding once live and once at its own capture point. The capture cannot\norder a binding declared in another function, so it fell back to a second\nlive evaluation. Only primitive results were kept, so every level of\nnesting doubled the work: 20 nested closures over a non-constant value\ntook about 14 s, and a 443 KB generated wrapper reached the 300 s\nanalysis timeout.\n\nKeep a live binding value when no caller could change it: no alias cycle\ncloses on or above the binding and the expression depth budget was not\nreached. Bindings on a cycle are still re-evaluated, so their values and\nreasons are unchanged.\n\nFound while building a source-owned reproduction for #1847; that report's\nworker heap exhaustion is a separate, already closed defect.\n\n* fix(javascript): keep the expression depth budget for retained values\n\nReusing a retained composite value from any depth let a chain of wrappers\n(s1 = {x: s0}, s2 = {x: s1}, ...) nest past the expression depth budget:\n300 wrappers produced a 299-level value where the budget stops at 86, the\nserialized IR grew quadratically, and 3,000 wrappers overflowed the stack\nin JSON.stringify.\n\nRecord the expression depth at which each caller-independent value was\nderived and reuse it only at that depth or shallower. A deeper reader\nderives the value again and reaches the budget exactly as before. Closure\naliases read their outer binding at one depth on both paths, so nested\nclosures stay linear.",
