      "sha": "c59fbf5e9c3b856d042f02cb18ad4de077eaeab5",
      "subject": "fix(artifacts): bound archive decompression by source size (#2144)",
      "body": "fix(artifacts): bound archive decompression by source size (#2144)\n\n* fix(artifacts): bound archive decompression by source size\n\nA ZIP/APK/IPA/MSIX member is attacker-controlled and can expand far\nbeyond the archive that carries it. Inventory hashed every member to EOF\nand extraction wrote every member with no ceiling, so a ~2 MiB archive\ncould drive ~30 s of CPU during inventory and ~150 s plus 2 GiB of disk\nwrites during extraction.\n\nA fixed per-entry byte cap is the wrong shape: it rejects legitimately\nlarge members inside large packages. The ceiling instead scales with the\narchive's own size (500x, with a 256 MiB floor), so large packages keep a\nproportionally large budget while a small archive cannot amplify into\nunbounded work. The cumulative declared size is charged from the central\ndirectory before any entry is decompressed, and zip.js already verifies\nstreamed output against each declaration, so the check is exact and\ncovers both inventory and extraction through the shared reader.\n\n* fix(artifacts): own decompression accounting per traversal\n\n* test(artifacts): verify declared ZIP output enforcement\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: baron <richstyles@gmail.com>",
