      "sha": "ab62ee9dcf98e4717d0b3445330f7cfdfe50c9f4",
      "subject": "fix(windows): preserve caller affinity only within one processor group (#2202)",
      "body": "fix(windows): preserve caller affinity only within one processor group (#2202)\n\n* fix(windows): preserve caller affinity only within one processor group\n\n`spawnProcess` read the caller's affinity mask and applied it to the\nsuspended child unconditionally. A mask names processors relative to one\nprocessor group, and Windows assigns each new process to a group of its\nown choosing, so on hosts with more than 64 logical processors the child\nis often placed in another group. `SetProcessAffinityMask` then rejects\nthe mask with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER (87) and the spawn fails, making\nevery owned provider process start intermittently on such hosts.\n\nAffinity is a nicety, not an authority or isolation control, so it is now\napplied only when the caller and the suspended child both occupy the same\nsingle processor group. A child placed in another group keeps that group's\ndefault affinity and the spawn proceeds. Job Object membership,\nkill-on-owner-close policy, owner normalization and priority preservation\nare unchanged, and the mask failure remains fatal within a shared group,\nwhere it is not expected.\n\nSingle-group hosts, including every host with 64 or fewer logical\nprocessors, are unaffected: `GetProcessGroupAffinity` reports one group\nfor both processes and the same mask is applied as before.\n\nThe optional process-boundary fixture gains an `affinity` observation,\nand the native verification lane asserts that an owned spawn succeeds and\nthat the child's mask matches the caller's whenever both share a single\ngroup.\n\nFixes #2181\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(windows): refuse to widen a pinned caller's affinity across groups\n\nAddresses review feedback on the first commit.\n\nAn unconstrained caller loses nothing when Windows places an owned child\nin another processor group, so that spawn proceeds with the group's\ndefault. A caller deliberately pinned to fewer processors than its group\nholds is a different case: silently accepting the default would hand the\nprovider tree the whole group, which `docs/installation.md` tells\noperators to rely on affinity to prevent. Such a spawn now fails naming\nthe constraint instead. `GetProcessAffinityMask` already reports the\ngroup's active set, which distinguishes the two without another query.\n\nSelecting the caller's group at creation through\nPROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_GROUP_AFFINITY was tried first and rejected:\n`CreateProcessW` fails with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER on a host with a\nsingle processor group, both alongside PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST and\non its own, so it cannot carry the ordinary path.\n\nThe boundary fixture's affinity observation now reports a process id's\nown mask rather than its own, because a separate probe process is\nassigned its own group and cannot stand in for the process that spawns\nthe child. Masks and the group's active set cross as fixed-width\nhexadecimal strings: a mask can set bits above the safe-integer range,\nwhere `JSON.parse` would round two different masks to one value.\n\nThe verification lane asserts the mask is inherited exactly when caller\nand child share a group, and otherwise that the caller was unconstrained.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(windows): record what owned-process affinity does not preserve\n\nA mask covering its group's whole active set does not distinguish a\ncaller the system placed in that group from one that selected the group\ndeliberately, so an owned child can still be placed elsewhere with its\nprocessor count bounded and its group placement lost. Treating that case\nas constrained instead is not available: on a multi-group host an\nordinary process is assigned a group by the system and its mask is that\ngroup's active set, so refusing it would fail every owned spawn there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
      "parents": ["2c6a4fac4ff3477ac14f3d780894c5b390880607"],
