    {
      "sha": "bf34d0d2fc3cc22076f49d682e74a0b71aa1db18",
      "subject": "perf(native): add opt-in Mach-O summary delivery and retained pages (#2634)",
      "body": "perf(native): add opt-in Mach-O summary delivery and retained pages (#2634)\n\n* perf(native): add opt-in Mach-O summary delivery and retained pages\n\ninspect_macho returns every import and export inline. For VS Code's\nElectron Framework one MCP call carries 2.5 MB of text (about 828k o200k\ntokens), and the same again as structuredContent; the MCP response\nbudget admits it. Agents asking for slice identity, UUID or dependency\ncounts paid that cost on every first call.\n\nAdd the established opt-in detail selection: omitted or complete keeps\nthe existing complete result and Evidence identity, while summary\nretains the complete Evidence and returns slice identity with\nper-collection returned/total/exhaustive counts (956 tokens).\ninspect_analysis_view kind macho pages the original rows of any\ncollection or the command provenance, repeating the producer's own\ntotal and exhaustive flag so page exhaustion is never read as absence.\n\nDelivery detail never reaches the native tools or the source Evidence\nID: registerEvidenceTools gains a parametersForEvidence hook, so the\nsummary's parent is the same Evidence as a complete call.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): refuse unretainable summary delivery before analysis\n\nA server without Evidence retention (a fixed analysis source) ran the\ncomplete inspect_macho or inspect_artifact analysis for detail summary\nand only then refused it in summarizeRetainedAnalysis, paying the very\ncost summary delivery exists to avoid. registerEvidenceTools gains a\npreflight hook; both registrations check retention there, as the WASM,\nEVM, managed, pcap and capture handlers already do before dispatch.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(native): keep whole rows in Mach-O architecture and segment pages\n\nThe page item union listed plain-object row arrays, and zod returns the\nfirst union member that parses while dropping unknown keys. Entrypoint\nrows declare only file_offset, so every architecture and segment row\n(which also carry a numeric file_offset) matched it and lost its name,\nCPU fields, permissions and sections. Rows now use exact key sets, and\nimports/exports share one member. The MCP test pages every facet of a\nreal /bin/ls and compares it with the unparsed complete rows.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
      "parents": ["e802441bce7674f18c847c2763ba6297809d5eff"],
