      "sha": "96fc84f1fb7c8bc69f66cd899896f4de9061fead",
      "subject": "fix(native): select one slice when universal Mach-O slices share a CPU type (#2040)",
      "body": "fix(native): select one slice when universal Mach-O slices share a CPU type (#2040)\n\n* fix(native): select one slice when universal Mach-O slices share a CPU type\n\nOn macOS 27 most system binaries are universal files whose slices share\nCPU_TYPE_ARM64: arm64e + arm64e.x1 (Calculator, TextEdit), x86_64 + arm64e +\narm64e.x1 (/bin/ls), or arm64 + arm64e.x1 (ShortcutDroplet). Target admission\nnames slices by CPU family, so these targets have availableArchitectures\n[\"arm64\"] while the file holds two arm64 slices.\n\ninspect_macho read that family count as a slice count and ran otool, nm,\ndyld_info, dwarfdump and vtool without -arch. ShortcutDroplet failed with\n\"Malformed otool load-command output\" (40 load commands against ncmds 20).\nCalculator reported both slices' imports (4138 instead of 2069), nm's\n\"(for architecture ...)\" header lines as exports, and no selected slice.\nselectArchitecture now also filters when lipo lists more than one slice. Its\npreference is unchanged: the family name, then arm64e, then the first\narm64e.<variant>.\n\ninspect_native_dispatch_metadata matched FAT slices by CPU type only and\nrefused /bin/ls and TextEdit as \"Ambiguous FAT architecture slice\".\nselectMachoSlice now ranks slices of the requested CPU type by base subtype in\nthe same order: arm64 (0, then 1) or x86_64 (3), then arm64e (2), then other\nvariants such as arm64e.x1 in FAT table order. It reports ambiguity only when\nthe top-ranked slices share a subtype.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(native): share Mach-O slice preference across inspections\n\n* chore: preserve upstream process capture test during merge\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
