      "sha": "9928f357fb9c420e6384e155d74586dfe37d4533",
      "subject": "fix(artifacts): read ASCII control bytes in legacy ZIP names as ASCII (#2365)",
      "body": "fix(artifacts): read ASCII control bytes in legacy ZIP names as ASCII (#2365)\n\n* fix(artifacts): read ASCII control bytes in legacy ZIP names as ASCII\n\nZIP names without the UTF-8 flag were decoded with zip.js's CP437 table,\nwhich maps bytes 0x01-0x1F and 0x7F to IBM PC display glyphs. A folder\nzipped on macOS with a custom icon therefore listed and extracted\n`Folder/Icon\\r` as `Folder/Icon♪`, a name that is not in the archive and\ndiffers from REA's directory inventory of the same folder. Tabs became `○`\nand DEL became `⌂`.\n\nDecode legacy names in REA's existing decodeText hook with the Unicode\nCP437 mapping that Python's zipfile also uses: bytes below 0x80 as ASCII,\nand the upper half with the same table zip.js uses.\n\nWindows file names cannot contain U+0001-U+001F, so a Windows extraction\ndestination now refuses such a path with a typed path failure before\ncreating anything, as it already does for ':'.\n\n* test(artifacts): check extracted legacy ZIP control names on disk\n\nThe control-name cases checked only the logical path that inventory\nreports. Extraction writes through SafeOutputTree, so a change that\nrenamed the extracted files would still pass those cases.\n\nExtract `Icon\\r` and `é\\t\\u007F.a`, then compare the reported\nrelative_path, the directory listing, and the file contents. The new\ncases skip on Windows, which refuses these names.\nsafeOutputTree.test.ts covers that refusal.",
      "parents": ["d13c9f840d36a321a56917e75c48afcb8d72920e"],
      "authors": [
