      "sha": "f974353c329ebfa58ba5f268078da30952a82ec0",
      "subject": "fix(javascript): keep paths after __dirname inside the source directory (#2366)",
      "body": "fix(javascript): keep paths after __dirname inside the source directory (#2366)\n\nNode's path.join does not reset at an absolute segment, so\npath.join(__dirname, '/preload.js') is <dir>/preload.js. The static path\nprojection skipped the __dirname anchor and joined the remaining parts,\nwhich turned that common spelling into the root path /preload.js. In an\napp whose main script lives in src/, the BrowserWindow preload was then\nreported not-found, or resolved to an unrelated preload.js at the artifact\nroot, and the utilityProcess.fork target was never resolved. String\nconcatenation had the same defect: path.dirname(__filename) +\n'/preload.js' projected to /preload.js, and __dirname + '/preload.js'\nstayed unknown.\n\nA source directory now contributes \".\" to a join, so path.join(__dirname)\nprojects to \".\" like path.resolve(__dirname). A concatenation that starts\nwith __dirname or the source file's dirname keeps text that begins with a\nseparator inside that directory; other text extends the directory's own\nname and stays unknown. The empty-string projection of\npath.dirname(__filename), which produced the wrong concatenation, is\nremoved, so a bare path.dirname(__filename) is unknown like a bare\n__dirname.",
