    {
      "sha": "8823c55b0ce325cf9edb7d2f9aadaf39db4e8f31",
      "subject": "feat(ghidra): decode Swift String literals in function dossiers (#2049)",
      "body": "feat(ghidra): decode Swift String literals in function dossiers (#2049)\n\n* feat(ghidra): decode Swift String literals in function dossiers\n\nSwift passes a String as two words. Small literals keep their UTF-8 bytes\ninside those words, and immortal large literals keep a count plus a pointer\nto their bytes biased by 32. Ghidra creates no data reference for either, so\nanalyze_function reported no strings for Swift code.\n\nOn AArch64 programs that contain Swift metadata, the Ghidra bridge now runs\nconstant propagation over the function and decodes register pairs where\nSwift consumes a String: consecutive argument registers at calls and tail\ncalls, x0 and x1 at returns, and both registers of one STP. Large literals\nmust resolve to terminated bytes inside one initialized block that is not\nexecutable. A pair whose registers both come from conditional selects is\nskipped, because Ghidra forks each select without correlating the\ncondition and could otherwise pair words from different cases.\n\nDecoded values are a derivation from the ABI layout rather than observed\nreferences, so they appear in a new optional swift_string_literals field\nwith their source instruction, registers and raw words, and in a matching\ninspect_analysis_view facet. referenced_strings is unchanged.\n\nRefs #2030\n\n* fix(ghidra): bound Swift literal reads and compare decoded strings\n\nLarge immortal literals are measured against the remaining heap before\nallocation, and a failed fit stays visible as a limitation. The canonical\nempty string is kept. Small and large literals now require matching\naddress nullability, and function comparison treats decoded literals as\nstring evidence without equating a missing record to an empty one.\n\n* fix(ghidra): budget Swift literal decoding and tighten use sites\n\nLarge literals now draw on one heap budget per dossier, measured once\nbefore decoding, instead of a separate check per literal. A literal that\ndoes not fit is listed in a new swift_string_literal_omissions field\nwith its address, byte length and reason, so consumers read omissions\nfrom structured data rather than from limitation text.\n\nstrings_names comparison checks referenced strings and names first and\nreports a change there even when Swift literal coverage differs; it\nreturns unknown only when those facts match and literal coverage is\nabsent on one side or partial.\n\nA computed jump counts as a tail call only when it has no resolved\ntarget inside the function, so jump tables no longer qualify, and a\nreturn requires a RETURN p-code operation, so traps no longer qualify.\n\nDecoded literals keep control characters, which Swift strings may\ncontain and JSON escapes. The Swift verifier builds the literal fixture\nfor arm64 on any Mac host and now also covers the empty string and a\nliteral containing a tab.\n\n* fix(ghidra): charge escaped JSON and follow copied selects\n\nThe Swift literal budget now charges the worst case per UTF-8 byte: UTF-16\ntext, six escaped JSON characters held in Gson's growing buffer and in the\nreturned String, and the UTF-8 bytes written to the socket. Literals made\nof control characters can no longer exceed the reserved heap.\n\nA jump counts as a tail call only when every resolved target lies outside\nthe function. The conditional select check follows register copies back\nto their definition, so selects copied into argument registers are\nrecognized like selects written there directly.\n\nstrings_names reports a change when both dossiers decoded different\nliterals at the same use site, even when either side has omissions; it\nreturns unknown only when no such use site disagrees.\n\n* fix(ghidra): bound literal records and trace selects across branches\n\nEvery Swift literal record now pays a fixed cost against the dossier\nbudget in addition to its text, so many empty or small literals are\nbounded as well. The first record that does not fit is kept as an\nomission and decoding stops there, which keeps the omission record\nitself bounded; omissions now describe small or large literals.\n\nThe conditional select check searches every predecessor path for the\nreaching definitions, following register copies, so selects made\nbefore a branch into the use block are recognized.\n\nA dossier records Swift literals and their omissions together. Coverage\nreads omissions first, and strings_names compares every use site that\nneither side omitted: a decoded literal is known where it was recorded,\nand an absence is known only in a dossier whose decoding examined every\nuse site.\n\n* fix(ghidra): stop every flow at the literal budget and route omissions\n\nEnding one flow from the evaluator leaves other queued flows running, so\nthe budget now records a shared stop that every flow checks before it\nexamines another instruction. A record that exceeds the budget is routed\nby that capacity result rather than by whether text was decoded, so a\nsmall literal over budget becomes an omission instead of a literal.\n\nWhen coverage is partial, the per site comparison keeps each literal's\nrepresentation as well as its value, matching the full strings_names\nprojection.\n\n* fix(ghidra): keep unresolved Swift tail-call sites unknown\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
