      "sha": "91830f1e0e68232e991902b0fa7617651d2eee15",
      "subject": "feat(managed): name the declaring type of call and field targets (#2163)",
      "body": "feat(managed): name the declaring type of call and field targets (#2163)\n\n* feat(managed): name the declaring type of call and field targets\n\nCall edges named external targets by member name alone (Join, ToJson,\nWriteAllText), and method-spec edges had no name at all, so an analyst could\nnot tell which library a method calls or find every use of a type without a\ndecompiler. Field accesses through MemberRefs had no name either.\n\nName each TypeDef, TypeRef, and generic-instantiation TypeSpec once, in the\nnamespace-qualified form TypeDef full_name uses, and add member_refs\nparent_name, call_edges target_declaring_type, and field_accesses\nfield_declaring_type. A method-spec target reports the generic method it\ninstantiates. Fields, member references, methods, and method specs now\nresolve through one token-keyed MemberCore lookup instead of three maps.\nEvidence recorded before these fields reads them as null.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): validate spec blobs and member kinds before naming targets\n\nAddress review findings on declaring-type naming:\n\n- A generic instantiation must name a TypeDef or TypeRef, so a TypeSpec\n  base is reported as malformed instead of followed. Naming no longer\n  recurses.\n- TypeSpec and MethodSpec instantiation blobs are decoded with the\n  member-signature grammar. Malformed blobs and out-of-range TypeDef rows\n  are reported against their owning row and make coverage partial.\n- A MethodSpec reads both columns. An invalid Method coded index is\n  reported; a readable Method keeps its name beside a malformed\n  instantiation.\n- MemberCore carries its signature kind, so a field operand is named only\n  by a field or field-signature MemberRef. This replaces the token-table\n  guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): validate MethodSpec arity and report unsupported spec blobs\n\n- A MethodSpec now checks that its method signature is generic and that\n  the instantiation supplies as many type arguments as it declares. A\n  non-generic target, a field reference, or a count mismatch is reported\n  as invalid-row; the readable method keeps its name.\n- Unsupported TypeSpec and MethodSpec forms raise an owner-scoped\n  unsupported-signature issue instead of reading as an intentional\n  unknown.\n- MethodSpec reading moves to ManagedMethodSpecs.ts, and its tests get\n  their own describe block.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): reject non-method signature headers and invalid TypeRef scopes\n\nThe method signature header reader now owns the calling-convention rule\nthat only FNPTR applied: FIELD, LOCAL_SIG, PROPERTY, and GENERICINST\nheaders, the reserved bit, and EXPLICITTHIS without HASTHIS are malformed.\nA MethodSpec whose method has a PROPERTY header is no longer accepted as a\ngeneric method, and member signatures with such headers no longer decode\nas methods.\n\nThe type-name lookup validates a TypeRef's ResolutionScope coded index and\nreports an out-of-range scope as invalid-row, keeping the readable name.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): hold member rows to MethodDefSig and FieldSig headers\n\nMethod signature headers are now checked in context. MethodDef and\nMemberRef rows accept only the MethodDefSig/MethodRefSig headers: DEFAULT,\nVARARG, or GENERIC with DEFAULT. FNPTR keeps the unmanaged conventions\ncompilers emit for function pointers. A field signature must be exactly\nFIELD, so a flagged FIELD header is malformed and never names a field.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): accept MethodSpec arity only from a fully decoded signature\n\nThe arity check read only the target's signature header, so a truncated\ngeneric method signature passed. Under type selection a MethodDef target\noutside the selection is never decoded elsewhere, so coverage stayed\ncomplete. The arity now comes from a transient full decode, and only a\ndecoded method signature supplies it. signature() keeps its raw length\nand digest around the shared decodeSignature.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(managed): decode each MethodSpec target signature once\n\nedgeTargets caches the generic arity, including a failed decode, by\ntarget method token, so many instantiations of one generic method share\na single signature decode. Unity.InputSystem has 472 distinct MethodSpecs\nover about 133 target methods.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): validate spec type operands and transitive reference scopes\n\n* fix(managed): retain readable names when a scope row is unreadable\n\n* test(managed): pin owner-scoped spec operand and scope-chain checks\n\ne59217c83 validates spec type operands inside specSignature, so a\nmalformed generic TypeSpec base is now invalid-row under its owning\nTypeSpec row; the three stale expectations follow that. New cases cover\na TypeSpec argument and a MethodSpec argument that name a missing TypeRef,\nand a TypeRef whose ResolutionScope names itself. The scope cases move to\ntheir own describe block.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): keep field targets from naming MethodSpecs\n\nA MethodSpec over a field-signature MemberRef inherited kind \"field\", so\nan ldsfld on that MethodSpec published a field name and declaring type\nfor malformed IL. A MethodSpec now borrows its target's name only when\nthe target is not a field; the arity check still reports it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): reuse owner-decoded signatures for MethodSpec arity\n\nMemberRef signatures, and MethodDef signatures in the type selection, are\nalready fully decoded, but the MethodSpec arity check decoded them again\nand admitted a second transient allocation, which could exhaust the\nbudget. MemberCore now carries the owner's genericArity, and only a target\nnothing decoded, such as an unselected MethodDef, falls back to the raw\nsignature. signatureGenericArity is the one rule for both paths.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
