    {
      "sha": "cae7620b77ec2893c944197603cc9158e2224930",
      "subject": "fix(apple): report a recognized connector that cannot name its owner (#2484)",
      "body": "fix(apple): report a recognized connector that cannot name its owner (#2484)\n\n* fix(apple): report a connector that cannot name its owner as an omission\n\nA connector is recognized by class name and then dropped by an early\n`continue` when its owner cannot be resolved. Nothing recorded the drop, so\nconnection coverage derived `complete` with `examined: 0` and `omitted: 0`\nfor a connector that was present, recognized and discarded.\n\nBoth producers of that coverage contract are affected and are changed\ntogether, because they share the facet and the reason value:\n\n- the keyed-archive parser records the drop as `unresolvedConnections` and\n  reports it through the `connections:<path>` facet, the document\n  `connection_count` and the graph omission total;\n- the NIBArchive projection in the artifact adapter recorded nothing at all,\n  and the projected dictionary carried no trace of the dropped connector for\n  a downstream consumer to find. Its count is taken where the connector is\n  discarded and plumbed through the same per-document omission channel that\n  already carries incomplete hierarchies, so the reason follows the cause.\n\nAn unresolved source cannot be represented as a connection at all, because\nconnection identity requires a non-empty source; that is unlike an unresolved\ndestination, which the graph already retains as an unresolved edge.\n`connection_limit_reached` stays reserved for connections dropped by the hard\ncap and `serialized_view_hierarchy_incomplete` for hierarchy omissions.\n\n`connection_count` also includes the unresolved connectors. That field already\ncounts connectors the parser recognized but could not emit, because\nconnections dropped by the hard cap are counted there too, so unresolved\nconnectors follow the existing rule rather than a new one.\n\nVerified with 98 focused Interface Builder and catalog tests, check:fast and\nverify:test-discovery on macOS arm64, and through the public CLI: a rooted\narchive holding one ownerless UIRuntimeOutletConnection reported complete with\nexamined 0 and omitted 0 before this change and reports partial with the reason\nconnection_unresolved and omitted 1 after it.\n\n* fix(apple): count ownerless NIBArchive connectors and keep the limit reason\n\nGreptile found two defects in the NIBArchive half of this change.\n\nThe document summary was left unchanged, so the same recognized connector\ncounted as one in a keyed archive and zero in a NIBArchive. The dictionary\nparser only sees connectors that were projected, so the omission recorded\nwhere the connector was dropped is added to the matching document here.\n\nThe facet reason was overwritten unconditionally, so a NIBArchive holding both\na connection dropped by the limit and an ownerless connector reported\nconnection_unresolved and lost the cause a higher limit could recover. The\nexisting reason is kept when the graph already named one, which is the\nprecedence the keyed-archive producer applies.\n\nVerified with `npm run test:focused` over the bundle suite (42 passed, 1\nskipped), `npm run lint` (0 errors) and `npm run check:fast` on macOS arm64.\nReverting the reason precedence alone fails only the limit case; reverting the\ndocument count fails the count assertion in both cases.\n\nThe new case moved into its own describe block because the enclosing callback\ncrossed the repository's max-lines-per-function bound otherwise.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: 吴佳浩 <wujiahao@supremind.com>",
