    {
      "sha": "a4b8e9ccee50f83bfb72d7ad33db6962c9befa72",
      "subject": "feat(ghidra): admit recordless legacy MIPS32 ELFs with a pinned 32-bit language (#1898)",
      "body": "feat(ghidra): admit recordless legacy MIPS32 ELFs with a pinned 32-bit language (#1898)\n\n* feat(ghidra): admit recordless legacy MIPS32 ELFs with a pinned 32-bit language\n\nFirmware built with pre-ABI-flags GNU toolchains declares EF_MIPS_ARCH_32\nwith an unspecified ABI field and no PT_MIPS_ABIFLAGS record. The lane\nrefused them before provider selection, and Ghidra's own header\nauto-selection picks a 64-bit R6 language for exactly these files.\n\nAdmit ELF32 ET_EXEC headers with EF_MIPS_ARCH_32 or EF_MIPS_ARCH_32R2,\nan unspecified ABI field, EF_MIPS_ABI2 clear and no observed ABI flags\nrecord. o32 is recorded as an inferred analysis assumption\n(mips_abi_basis), floating-point ABI semantics stay unverified, and the\nexisting refusals (FP64, NAN2008, ASEs, machine variants, ELF64) are\nunchanged. The profile pins -processor MIPS:<LE|BE>:32:default through a\nprovider-pinned-v1 selection the bridge-reported language_id is checked\nagainst after import; a caller-selected REA_GHIDRA_LANGUAGE_ID still\nwins. The lane identity is versioned\nelf32-exec-arch32-legacy-pinned-v1.\n\nverify:ghidra:mips gains a recordless legacy round for both byte orders\nthat strips the ABI record declarations from a real mips32 fixture,\nchecks REA's interpretation against GNU readelf, asserts the pinned\nlanguage after import and the retained assumption limitations through a\nsnapshot round-trip.\n\nFixes #1620\n\n* fix(ghidra): require an inspected absence for the recordless MIPS lane\n\nAddress review: undefined ABI flags means the record was not inspected,\nnot that none exists. Only an inspected absence (null) establishes the\nrecordless legacy admission; a merely unobserved record keeps its\nspecific refusal. Also document the legacy admission and its pinned\nlanguage in the MIPS provider guide.\n\n* fix(ghidra): tighten the recordless MIPS admission boundaries\n\nAddress review: a recordless-looking header whose ABI-record inspection\nleft declaration sources unexamined (carved ELF with a partial section\ntable and no program-header record) no longer establishes the recordless\nadmission; it is refused with the inspection limitation retained. An\nEF_MIPS_ARCH_32 header that declares an ABI is refused rather than\nrelabeled through the 32R2 declared-ABI lane.\n\n* fix(ghidra): reject EF_MIPS_ABI_ON32 from the recordless MIPS lane\n\nAddress review: bit 0x40 declares the old n32 ABI. A recordless header\ncarrying it was admitted with the inferred-o32 assumption even though\nthe header itself declares n32; the recordless shape now requires the\nbit clear and refuses it with a specific constraint.\n\n* fix(ghidra): keep recordless relocatable MIPS objects off the legacy lane\n\nThe recordless admission now requires ET_EXEC: the lane and its\nverification cover executable firmware images, and a recordless ET_REL\nobject must stay on the declared-ABI path instead of inheriting the\ninferred-o32 assumption under an executable lane id. The ABI-record\nrequirement paragraph is scoped to the declared-ABI lane so it no longer\ncontradicts the recordless admission documented above it.\n\n* fix(ghidra): name the ET_EXEC scope for recordless objects and probe the legacy lane\n\nA recordless relocatable object is not an incomplete inspection, so its\nrefusal now names the ET_EXEC scope of the legacy admission instead of\nclaiming unavailable ABI headers remain unknown. The recordless legacy\nverifier round reads the probe pair and applies the full assertMipsProbe\noracle like the standard round, and its report keeps the independent\nGNU readelf receipt beside the fixture facts.\n\n* fix(ghidra): retain the concrete ELF32 legacy compiler pin\n\n* fix(ghidra): bind legacy profiles to fully admitted targets\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: BaaRai <baarai@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
