    {
      "sha": "a3daf8a07bf938ce6765b0e15c527493f9496a9f",
      "subject": "fix(ci): run the Windows native lane with its process boundary fixture (#2336)",
      "body": "fix(ci): run the Windows native lane with its process boundary fixture (#2336)\n\n* fix(ci): run the Windows native lane with its process boundary fixture\n\n`verify-windows-native.mjs` takes the boundary executable as its second\nargument and skips every observation that needs it when the argument is\nabsent. CI passed no argument, so the breakaway denial, caller and child\ntoken comparison, Windows environment semantics and in-place ancestor\nreparse attempt have never run there; they only ran for an operator who\nbuilt the fixture by hand, which `native/windows/README.md` described as\noptional.\n\nThe fixture is an ordinary Windows executable independent of the addon,\nso the lane that already installs MinGW to cross-build the artifact\ncross-builds it too and uploads it beside the existing inspection\nfixture. The Windows job downloads it and passes its path, matching how\nthat inspection fixture already crosses the same job boundary.\n\nNo observation, assertion or threshold changes; this only stops four of\nthem from being silently skipped.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): pass the process boundary fixture in the real Ghidra Windows lane\n\n`real-ghidra-windows.yml` also runs the native lane without the boundary\nexecutable, so its proof run skipped the same breakaway, caller and child\ntoken, environment semantics and ancestor reparse observations. Its\nartifact job already installs MinGW, so it cross-builds and uploads the\nfixture the same way, and the self-hosted Windows job downloads it and\npasses its path.\n\nThis makes the README's claim about both Windows workflows true rather\nthan narrowing it to one lane.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(process): report no remaining time after a provider deadline fires\n\nNode timers can fire while performance.now() is still a fraction of a\nmillisecond short of the monotonic deadline. ProviderStartupDeadline then\nreported interruption \"timeout\" and an aborted signal while remainingMs()\nreturned ceil(0.x) = 1, so polling loops guarded by remainingMs() > 0\n(Hopper and Ghidra startup) could run past an elapsed deadline, and the\nreal-timer test \"aborts a bounded wait when its real monotonic deadline\nexpires\" failed intermittently in CI (expected 1 to be 0).\n\nOnce the deadline's own timer has classified a timeout, report zero\nremaining time. A deterministic regression test fires the timer with the\nmonotonic clock 0.4 ms short of the deadline.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n(cherry picked from commit dfdaa0ca74cc84a45dcb186422a0f51aa61bc427)\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <morluto@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Kaoru0822 <208281322+kaoru0822-kitauji@users.noreply.github.com>",
