      "sha": "7369b5ee63c69af8af18601bb1de92dd41c6a250",
      "subject": "fix(ida): retain cleanup facts and let one-shot commands exit (#2814)",
      "body": "fix(ida): retain cleanup facts and let one-shot commands exit (#2814)\n\n* fix(ida): let one-shot commands exit after retaining cleanup\n\nWhen a headless IDA request outlives timeoutMs (for example idb_open on\na large binary still running auto-analysis), REA cannot prove the\nworker stopped and correctly retains the upstream connection and\nprivate workspace. The retained stdio child and its pipes stayed\nreferenced, so a one-shot CLI printed its cleanup_incomplete result and\nthen never exited, keeping the supervisor, worker and database alive.\n\nDetach the retained connection's child process and pipes from the event\nloop when cleanup fails. A long-lived MCP server can still retry\nthrough the retained connection; a one-shot CLI exits with status 1,\nand the supervisor observes end of input.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): report an unverified upstream worker after failed cleanup\n\nUpstream ida-pro-mcp spawns headless workers in their own session and\ndeliberately leaves them running when its supervisor exits, so neither\na retained connection nor REA exiting proves that a timed-out worker\nstopped. When cleanup fails before the owned worker's release is\nconfirmed, the cleanup diagnostics now name its private session and\nstate that it may still be running until it finishes and reaches its\nidle timeout. Failures after a confirmed release, or before any open\nwas attempted, do not report a worker.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): name the inventory sessions that block worker release\n\nWhen idb_close confirmed the requested worker gone but the follow-up\ninventory still found a different peer on the private input, the\nupstream_worker diagnostic named the released session and could\nmisdirect manual cleanup. Record the session ids of the inventory\nentries that actually blocked release and report those; fall back to\nthe requested private session only when no inventory evidence exists\n(an incomplete open or an unanswered request). The field becomes\nsession_ids, since more than one entry can block release.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): keep blocker evidence and backend in cleanup diagnostics\n\nThe release diagnostic reset its observed blockers on every close, so a\nretry whose inventory was unavailable fell back to the already-released\nsession; and it kept only session ids, describing a blocking GUI\nsession with detached-worker guidance.\n\nBlocker evidence is now replaced only by a successful inventory, and\nthe diagnostic becomes upstream_sessions with each session's id and\nbackend, with guidance that distinguishes workers from GUI sessions.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): stop naming a worker after its close was confirmed\n\nWhen idb_close confirmed the requested worker's release but the\nfollowing inventory failed, no blocker evidence existed yet and the\ndiagnostic fell back to the confirmed-absent worker. After a confirmed\nclose the diagnostic now reports an empty session list, meaning any\nremaining same-input sessions are unknown.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ida): group cleanup diagnostic cases in their own suite\n\nThe added cases pushed the reaped-worker describe block past the\n150-line limit enforced by lint:built.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): keep ownership and pre-close peers in cleanup diagnostics\n\nBlocking sessions dropped the parsed ownership fact, so an external\nunowned worker looked the same as REA's own; and recovery inventory\nrecorded same-input peers only when the requested worker was missing,\nso a peer seen beside it was lost if the following close failed.\n\nEach reported session now carries owned (null when no inventory has\nreported it), and recovery records every same-input session until a\nlater successful inventory replaces that evidence.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): drop a confirmed worker from retained cleanup blockers\n\nRecovery inventory now records the requested worker beside any peers,\nso once idb_close confirmed its release a failing follow-up inventory\nstill reported it as unreleased. Remove the confirmed session from the\nretained blockers at close confirmation and keep the remaining peers.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ida): retry cleanup through a detached stdio connection\n\nThe retry coverage used a recording connection whose detach() only\ncounts calls, so it could not show that detaching keeps a retained\nconnection usable. Run a real stdio supervisor in a long-lived host,\nfail the first post-close inventory, and require the retry through the\nsame detached connection to confirm release and remove the workspace.\nThe test fails if detach() destroys the pipes instead of unref-ing them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): close the stdio supervisor in failed retry tests\n\nMerge main and give its new IdaNativeProtocol connection double the\nrequired detach() method. The real-stdio retry test now closes its\nconnection in an unconditional teardown, so a failing assertion cannot\nleave the supervisor waiting on stdin.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ida): share the cleanup diagnostic expectation in session tests\n\nRepeated upstream_sessions assertions and the unrelated live peer\nliteral pushed repository duplication over the jscpd threshold. Build\nthe expected diagnostic with one helper and reuse one peer constant.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ida): distinguish observed ownership from unreported input bindings\n\n* fix(ida): keep requested-ID conflicts in recovery cleanup\n\nRecovery inventory kept only same-input entries, unlike the startup and\npost-close filters. After an explicitly rejected open, an entry under\nthe requested session ID on another input was dropped, so cleanup could\nclose the connection, remove the private workspace and report success\nwhile that session stayed registered. Match the requested ID as well so\nthe entry is retained as a blocker and cleanup stays incomplete.\n\nAlso split the cleanup diagnostics suite under the lint:built function\nlength limit, and make two startup-inventory fixtures pass a defined\nsession id so the test file typechecks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ida): preserve retained session inventory facts\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Xingchuan Ma <xingchuan.ma@msn.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
