      "sha": "26525706fcd102355d1cd765138398067f32527f",
      "subject": "feat(managed): decode ldstr user strings in member inspection (#2162)",
      "body": "feat(managed): decode ldstr user strings in member inspection (#2162)\n\n* feat(managed): decode ldstr user strings in member inspection\n\ninspect_managed_members reported every ldstr operand as a bare 0x70 token,\nso an analyst could see that a method loads a literal but not which one.\nAnswering \"where is this path or key used\" required a second tool.\n\nRead the optional #US stream and add a top-level user_strings inventory:\neach distinct token loaded by an inspected method body, once, in token\norder, with its #US file offset and decoded UTF-16 value. Instruction\nanchors and normalized CIL digests keep their build-local tokens. A missing\n#US stream or malformed item is reported against its token as partial\ncoverage. Evidence recorded before this field reads user_strings as null.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): collect user strings only from ldstr\n\nA malformed ldtoken can carry a 0x70 token, which the operand decoder\nclassifies as a string operand. Only ldstr loads a literal, so collect\nuser strings by opcode.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): admit user-string tokens and failures before collecting them\n\nCharge each distinct ldstr token to the decode budget before adding it to\nthe collection set, and admit each #US failure before retaining it. A\nmissing #US stream is now one stream-level issue rather than one per\ntoken.\n\nDocument why the #US terminal flag is not validated: Mono and Roslyn\nassemblies disagree with ECMA-335 II.24.2.4 and the runtime ignores it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(managed): describe missing #US reporting and the unvalidated flag byte\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(managed): cover #US items that run past the stream end\n\nAn odd length whose payload crosses the end of #US, and a two-byte\nlength prefix that starts on the last #US byte, are both rejected as\n\"User string leaves #US\" beside a readable literal. The test fails if\nthat bounds check is removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): report reserved #US length prefixes as string failures\n\nThe compressed-length reader shared by #Blob and #US hard-coded\ninvalid-blob and \"Metadata blob\" for a reserved prefix, so a malformed\nuser string was published as a blob failure. The caller now names the\nitem it reads: #Blob keeps its code and message, and #US reports\ninvalid-string.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): append member inspection issues without argument spreading\n\ninspectManagedMembersBytes gathered every producer's issues with\nissues.push(...list), passing one argument per issue. A large malformed\ninput, such as hundreds of thousands of distinct bad ldstr operands or\nMemberRef rows, overflows the stack with an untyped RangeError instead of\nreturning partial coverage. All six sites now use\nappendManagedParseIssues, which appends one issue at a time.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): classify truncated #US and #Blob length prefixes by item\n\nA two- or four-byte compressed-length prefix cut off by the end of the\nartifact went through the generic range check and was reported as\ninvalid-row. The prefix reader now uses the item's own code for both\nreserved and truncated prefixes: invalid-string for #US, invalid-blob for\n#Blob. The unused generic range helper is removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): retain user-string terminal flag conformance issues\n\n* test(managed): write ECMA-335 #US terminal flags and cover flag issues\n\nd5b7b0704 reports a #US terminal flag that differs from ECMA-335\nII.24.2.4. The fixture heap always wrote 0, so its non-Latin-1 literals\nnow read as nonconforming. The fixture writes the spec flag, and a new\ntest flips one flag each way (\"ok\" set to 1, \"it's\" set to 0) and checks\nthat both literals remain beside one issue each.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): classify ldstr operands before collapsing a missing #US\n\nWith no #US stream, every collected ldstr operand was counted as a #US\nreference and reported only as missing-stream, while the same non-0x70\noperand got invalid-heap-index when the stream existed. parseUserStrings\nnow owns which operands are user-string tokens: any other operand is\ninvalid-heap-index either way, and only 0x70 tokens count toward the\nmissing-stream issue. The heap reader drops its duplicate kind check.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): state user-string limits in member comparisons\n\ncompare_managed_members ignored user_strings, so a method whose ldstr\nliteral changed at the same token, or legacy Evidence that never examined\nliterals, could read as unchanged with no caveat. The comparison now adds\nan unknown limitation when either side predates user strings and a\nnot-compared limitation when literals were observed. ExportedType and\nuser-string inventories share one uncomparedInventory rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(managed): advertise ldstr literals in member inspection help\n\nThe MCP tool description, the type_names input, and the CLI command and\n--type help now say that inspect_managed_members returns the #US string\nliterals that ldstr loads, and that a type selection limits them to the\nliterals the selected types' method bodies load.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): read the empty #US entry at offset 0\n\nECMA-335 II.24.2.4 makes the first #US entry the empty blob: a zero\nlength with no terminal flag byte. ldstr 0x70000000 loads it, but the\nodd-length check rejected it as invalid-string, dropping the literal and\nmaking coverage partial. Offset 0 with length 0 now reads as \"\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): reserve #US offset 0 and admit literals before scanning\n\nOffset 0 is the reserved empty #US entry, so a nonempty item there is now\ninvalid-string instead of a published literal. The literal's projection\nis reserved before the terminal-flag scan, so an inadmissible item fails\nat the budget without reading every code unit. The scan's loop index no\nlonger shadows the token's heap index.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): report #US length prefixes wider than ECMA-335 assigns\n\ncompressedLength accepted an overlong encoding, such as 0x80 0x05 for a\n5-byte item, without comment. It now reports whether the width is the\none ECMA-335 II.23.2 assigns, and the #US reader reports a non-minimal\nprefix as an invalid-string conformance issue beside the readable\nliteral, like the terminal flag. readMetadataUserString returns every\nissue it observes. #Blob behavior is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): check the #US length width before the empty entry\n\nThe offset-0 empty entry returned \"\" before the length-width check, so an\noverlong empty entry such as 0x80 0x00 read as complete. The width issue\nis now collected first and returned with the empty literal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): admit #US width issues only for returned literals\n\nThe overlong-prefix issue was reserved before the fatal shape checks, so\nan item that then failed, such as 0x80 0x00 away from offset 0, leaked\n512 bytes of budget per token. The width issue is now built and admitted\nonly on paths that return the literal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): keep #US issues out of member metadata status\n\nUser-string issues, including terminal-flag and length-prefix\nconformance differences, marked metadata.status partial, so\ncompare_managed_members turned one-sided methods and fields into unknown\neven though every member row was examined. #US issues now make coverage\npartial while metadata.status describes the member inventory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): give user strings their own coverage\n\n#US issues entered the member coverage, so every consumer that reads it\nas member coverage (application graph member facts, comparison status)\nreported examined members as partial over a literal conformance\ndifference. user_strings is now { state, literals, issues }: #US issues\nlive there and never change the member coverage or metadata.status.\nparseUserStrings returns that inventory directly.\n\ncompare_managed_members inspects with user strings skipped, since it\nnever reads literals, so a large literal cannot exhaust the decode budget\nof a comparison. Its limitations state once that literals are not\ncompared, and the ExportedType limitations return to their main form.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): mark user strings partial when a body is not fully decoded\n\nWith user strings owning their coverage, an inspected method body that\nwas partial, malformed, or truncated no longer reached their state, so a\nliteral after the decode stopped was missing from a \"complete\" inventory.\nparseUserStrings now marks the inventory partial unless every inspected\nbody was fully decoded, keeping the literals it did observe.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): mark user strings partial when a method row is omitted\n\nA MethodDef row that failed before its body was read was recorded as an\nissue and omitted, so its ldstr instructions were never scanned, yet the\nuser-string inventory could still read as complete. parseUserStrings now\ntakes the parseMethods result and stays partial whenever a method row\nfailed, as well as when an inspected body was not fully decoded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): count only method rows that may hide a selected body\n\nAny parseMethods issue marked user strings partial, including an\nunreadable name on a method outside the type selection, so a fully\nscanned selection read as incomplete. parseMethods now reads each row's\nowner first and reports unscannedRows: rows that produced no method while\ntheir owner is selected or unknown. parseUserStrings uses that count.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): treat unreadably named types as possibly selected\n\nUnder a type_names selection, a type whose name could not be read may be\na requested type, but its methods were skipped without counting toward\nunscannedRows, so an unexamined body left user strings complete. A\nmethod whose owner's name is unreadable now counts as possibly selected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): judge user-string scans by CIL decoding, not body status\n\nbody.status is malformed for an invalid exception section even when\nevery instruction decoded, so user strings read as partial although every\nldstr was examined. The body reader now reports whether its CIL was fully\nscanned, independent of exception-region validity, and parseMethods folds\nunscanned bodies and unproduced rows into one unscannedMethods count that\nparseUserStrings uses instead of reinterpreting body.status.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): stop deduplicating user-string issues\n\nThe inventory passed its issues through uniqueManagedParseIssues, which\nserializes every issue into an unbudgeted map: about 42 MB of heap and\n113 ms for 125,000 issues on Node 24. The issues are unique by\nconstruction (one failure or per-offset conformance issues per distinct\ntoken, one missing-stream issue), so the inventory now returns them, and\nits literals, without copies.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(managed): preserve selected string coverage unknowns\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
