    {
      "sha": "e1897c3c17c342cddf8834622c9d63e7bc6ff313",
      "subject": "fix(verify-package): read Hopper installer applicability from its owner (#2973)",
      "body": "fix(verify-package): read Hopper installer applicability from its owner (#2973)\n\n* fix(verify-package): read Hopper installer applicability from its owner\n\npackageSetupSupport derived hopperSetupSupported from the general doctor\nhost check plus a non-Windows condition, so a Debian-family host REA\nsupports but the Hopper package never tested reported an automated\ninstaller the packaged setup never offers. verifySetupApply then expected\nneeds_confirmation for the explicit --install-hopper request and failed\nat verify-package-setup.mjs:403.\n\nApplicability now comes from readLinuxDistribution() in the built\napplication, so the verifier follows the predicate the package ships, and\nit stays unknown when no distribution can be read instead of collapsing\ninto a known-unsupported host. Generic agent configuration and idempotence\nassertions stay independent of installer applicability, and the expected\nstatus is still derived from the host rather than from the request being\nasserted.\n\nVerified on Kali 2026.3, a Debian-family host with an installer-untested\ndistribution and the simulated Hopper package environment: before the\nchange npm run verify:package fails at verify-package-setup.mjs:403 with\nthe packaged setup correctly returning needs_human; after it the same run\npasses every stage. Both applicability branches are covered by\ntests/evaluation/package/packageSetupSupport.test.ts, which runs in the\nsource lane without a build. No real Hopper installation was exercised,\nso no engine compatibility claim follows.\n\nRefs #2939\n\n* fix(ci): preserve Hopper doctor readiness checks\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
