      "sha": "b9b3a9098b072313119eccb3ae574faa57c3e517",
      "subject": "fix(gemini): compare consolidated allowlists through one normalizer (#2495)",
      "body": "fix(gemini): compare consolidated allowlists through one normalizer (#2495)\n\n* fix(gemini): compare consolidated allowlists through one normalizer\n\ngeminiServerPolicyBlock consolidates allowlists by normalized intersection but\ntested the consolidated list against the raw server key. Because the\nintersection keeps the spelling of whichever policy came first, the same pair of\npolicies blocked the server in one arrangement and allowed it in the other, and\nthe excluded test had the mirror-image asymmetry.\n\nNormalize the comparison key once and use the same normalizer for the exclusion\ntest, the intersection and the membership test. The messages keep the\ncaller-facing server key, so lookup and display stay separate.\n\nVerified with 88 client profile, lifecycle and registration tests, check:fast\nand verify:test-discovery on macOS arm64. The regression test drives setup twice\nwith the same policies in both arrangements; before the change one arrangement\nfailed and the other passed.\n\n* test(gemini): cover normalized exclusions and padded allowlist entries\n\nThe regression case added with the fix drove the allowlist intersection in\nboth spellings but left the other two comparisons the change touches\nuncovered: the exclusion membership test and the trimming applied to padded\nnames. Reverting either behaviour kept the suite green, so neither was\npinned. Greptile raised exactly this on the PR.\n\nAn exclusion is now exercised as \" REA \" against the selected server, and a\npadded allowlist entry is exercised as the only policy, so a padded entry has\nto permit the same server its exact spelling permits rather than fall to the\nmembership test.\n\nVerified with `npm run test:focused -- tests/boundary/cli/clientProfiles.test.ts`:\n14 passed with the change; reverting only ClientConfigurationDocument.ts to\nmain fails both cases, the arrangement case with `rea+REA: expected 1 to be +0`\nand the new one on both the excluded and the padded-allowlist assertions.\n\n* fix(process): report no remaining time after a provider deadline fires\n\nNode timers can fire while performance.now() is still a fraction of a\nmillisecond short of the monotonic deadline. ProviderStartupDeadline then\nreported interruption \"timeout\" and an aborted signal while remainingMs()\nreturned ceil(0.x) = 1, so polling loops guarded by remainingMs() > 0\n(Hopper and Ghidra startup) could run past an elapsed deadline, and the\nreal-timer test \"aborts a bounded wait when its real monotonic deadline\nexpires\" failed intermittently in CI (expected 1 to be 0).\n\nOnce the deadline's own timer has classified a timeout, report zero\nremaining time. A deterministic regression test fires the timer with the\nmonotonic clock 0.4 ms short of the deadline.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: 吴佳浩 <wujiahao@supremind.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Kaoru0822 <208281322+kaoru0822-kitauji@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
