      "sha": "641a8395ee6e7f9fbf268765bb3e2ff3c2604dd2",
      "subject": "fix(cli): refuse a JavaScript integrity policy the binary analyze lane cannot honor (#2808)",
      "body": "fix(cli): refuse a JavaScript integrity policy the binary analyze lane cannot honor (#2808)\n\n* fix(cli): refuse an integrity policy the binary analyze lane cannot honor\n\n`analyze` declares --integrity-policy for its JavaScript lane and forwards it\nonly there. On a binary target the flag was accepted and then dropped:\ninspect_artifact-style integrity handling does not exist on this path, and\nbinary_overview is a strictObject with no input at all, so the request could\nnever take effect and the run proceeded as if the caller had not asked.\n\nThe same branch already refuses source_paths on a binary target with a typed\nAnalysisInputError, and the sibling `inspect` command refuses the flag outright,\nso the refusal is the established handling here. The policy option carries a\ndefault, so the check keys off a value that differs from the owner schema's own\ndefault rather than the flag's presence; otherwise every binary analyze would be\nrefused. The default is read from the schema instead of being restated.\n\nVerified with 5 integrity policy CLI cases, check:fast and\nverify:test-discovery (1023 files) on macOS arm64, and by running the built CLI\nfor the no-flag, explicit-default and record-and-continue cases. The regression\ncase failed with the command reaching the binary lane before the change.\n\n* fix(cli): reject explicit default integrity policies for binaries\n\n* fix(cli): keep an omitted integrity policy absent on the binary lane\n\n`javascriptApplicationOptions.shape.integrityPolicy` fills an omission with its\ndefault, and `.optional()` keeps reporting that parsed value, so\n`options.integrityPolicy !== undefined` was true for every `analyze <target>`\ncall: ordinary binary analysis was rejected before the target was even read.\n\nList `z.undefined()` first in a union so an omitted option stays absent while\nthe owner still validates a supplied value, and reapply the owner's metadata so\nhelp and `--schema` keep describing the option.\n\nValidation: tests/boundary/cli/javascriptIntegrityPolicyCli.test.ts reports\n1 failed | 5 passed without this change (the case \"still routes a binary\nanalyze target without an integrity policy\" returned invalid_request for\nintegrity_policy instead of reaching the target) and 6 passed with it;\n`npm run check:fast` passes on macOS 26.5.1 arm64 with Node.js 24.18.0.\n\n* fix(cli): omit integrity defaults from binary option schema\n\n* test(ci): isolate report fixtures and declare PowerPC Java bounds\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: 吴佳浩 <wujiahao@supremind.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>",
