      "sha": "77be0f6666df67de48ad737fe5b5d204aa34ae85",
      "subject": "fix(capture): end special-scheme URL authorities at a backslash (#2767)",
      "body": "fix(capture): end special-scheme URL authorities at a backslash (#2767)\n\n* fix(capture): end special-scheme URL authorities at a backslash\n\nThe native mitmproxy adapter removed transport userinfo with\n`^(...//)[^/?#]*@`, so the authority ran to the last \"@\" before \"/\", \"?\"\nor \"#\". For `https://example.test\\part@tail` it treated\n`example.test\\part` as userinfo, reported `https://tail` as the\nLocation, and withdrew the original bytes as a transport credential.\nWHATWG URL parsing, used by the shared TypeScript owner\n(recordedTransportUrl.ts) and by HAR captures, ends a special-scheme or\nscheme-relative authority at a backslash, so that \"@\" is ordinary path\ntext.\n\nLocate the authority with the same boundary rule before removing\nuserinfo. Bytes are handled as Latin-1 text so unrelated bytes stay\nidentical. Userinfo before the backslash is still removed and redacted.\nAdd source unit tests for the adapter projection to test:python.\n\nFixes #2671\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(capture): skip special-scheme leading slashes before userinfo\n\nEnding the authority at a backslash exposed a credential: for\n`https://\\user:pass@host/path`, WHATWG parsing skips every leading \"/\"\nor \"\\\" after a special scheme (and in scheme-relative references), so\n`user:pass` is userinfo, but the new boundary stopped at the first\nbackslash and found no \"@\". The same applied to `https:\\\\user:pass@host`\nand `https:user:pass@host`.\n\nStart the authority after that whole slash run for non-file special\nschemes and scheme-relative references. File URLs keep their \"//\"\nboundary because they carry no userinfo. Load the adapter in tests with\nfresh mitmproxy stand-ins scoped to its import instead of leaving them\nin sys.modules.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(capture): parse URL controls and mixed network authorities\n\n* fix(capture): distinguish native paths and URL base authorities\n\n* fix(capture): preserve file-relative path evidence after separators\n\n* fix(comparison): recheck historical calls-only changes (#2223)\n\n* fix(comparison): preserve incomplete function call evidence\n\nKeep missing, unknown, partial and unresolved call relationships unknown in function comparisons and reconstruction claims. Aggregate IDA caller and callee coverage without overwriting uncertainty.\n\nValidate with 144 focused tests, typecheck/lint, docs checks and test discovery. Real providers were not verified; the full headless IDA session suite requires unavailable Windows native controls.\n\n* fix(comparison): recheck historical calls-only changes\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* fix(android): make DEX descriptor pattern portable\n\n* fix(ci): isolate Linux Hopper process fixture inventory\n\n* fix(capture): remove empty URL userinfo markers\n\n* test(capture): cover empty URL userinfo markers\n\n* test(ci): isolate Hopper process inventory fixtures\n\n* fix(ci): declare process inventory callback\n\n* test(ci): isolate alternate launcher process fixture\n\n* fix(capture): keep URLs that WHATWG rejects instead of redacting userinfo\n\nFile URLs cannot carry userinfo (an \"@\" in a file host fails the parse),\nand a special authority whose host or port WHATWG rejects fails the whole\nURL. Neither has credentials, so the bridge now keeps their source text\nand byte sidecar. Checked against Node's URL over 30,000 spellings: no\nunder-redaction, over-redaction 20,540 -> 5,865 (remaining: empty\nuserinfo, IPv4 range, IDNA, non-special and baseless references).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(capture): strip trailing URL whitespace and validate IP hosts and ports\n\nThe previous host check counted trailing C0 controls and spaces, which\nWHATWG strips, so `https://user:pw@host ` kept its password. Trailing\nC0/space is now removed from the parse view (raw offsets are unchanged).\n\nSpecial hosts now also follow the WHATWG IPv4 parser (ends-in-a-number,\nhex/octal parts, range) and IPv6 literal syntax (zone IDs rejected), and\nports are bounded lexically before int(), which raised ValueError past\n4300 digits on Python 3.11+ and failed the whole capture.\n\nNode URL differential over 126,000 spellings: under-redaction 8,084 -> 0,\nValueError 2,550 -> 0; remaining special-scheme over-redaction is IDNA\nand the pinned empty-userinfo case. Cost: +0.6 us per URL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(capture): redact userinfo before hosts that need Unicode mapping\n\nA non-ASCII special host may map under UTS #46 to an accepted host\n(fullwidth digits, ideographic full stops, ignored code points), so\ntreat it as accepted instead of running the ASCII IPv4 check on it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: morluto <76467478+morluto@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Lucien <164857476+dakjdakd@users.noreply.github.com>",
