    {
      "sha": "3f5b7033799f9fe993ad5b81fb51c8f822173817",
      "subject": "fix(native): derive Mach-O word size and byte order from the selected slice (#3163)",
      "body": "fix(native): derive Mach-O word size and byte order from the selected slice (#3163)\n\n* fix(native): derive Mach-O word size and byte order from the selected slice\n\ninspect_macho read endian and word_size from `file -b` for the whole\nfile. macOS `file` never prints a Mach-O byte order, so endian was null\nfor every target. For a universal binary, word_size matched \"64-bit\"\nanywhere in the summary instead of describing the selected slice, and it\nwas null for a thin 32-bit slice.\n\nBoth now come from the selected slice's otool header: the magic gives\nthe word size and the CPU family gives the byte order (x86 and ARM\nlittle, PowerPC big, other families unknown). The file capture is no\nlonger needed by inspect_macho, so it is no longer run there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(native): select a 32-bit slice beside a 64-bit slice through inspect_macho\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
